1
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:20,230
[Cheerful instrumental music]

2
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:25,570
[Clock dinging]

3
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:56,220
[Children screaming]

4
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:58,260
All right, what's it going to be?

5
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:00,770
A Triple Cream Cup for Christopher.

6
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:03,430
A Squelchy Snorter for Otis.

7
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:06,690
A Sizzler for June Marie. And listen.

8
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:09,440
BILL: Wonka's got a new one today.
CHILDREN: What is it?

9
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,230
This is called a Scrumdiddleumptious Bar.

10
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:15,070
Scrumdiddleumptious Bar?
How does he do it?

11
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:17,990
- My boy, do you ask a fish how it swims?
- No.

12
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:19,660
BILL: Or a bird how it flies?

13
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,910
No sirree, you don't.
They do it because they were born to do it.

14
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,210
Just like Willy Wonka
was born to be a candy man...

15
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:29,170
and you look like
you were born to be a Wonkerer.

16
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:32,340
[Singing] Who can take the sunrise

17
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:35,340
Sprinkle it with dew

18
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:39,810
Cover it in chocolate and a miracle or two?

19
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,810
The Candy Man.

20
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,020
[Singing] The Candy Man can

21
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:49,440
The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love

22
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:51,900
And makes the world taste good

23
00:03:53,690 --> 00:03:56,860
Who can take a rainbow

24
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:59,030
Wrap it in a sigh

25
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:04,160
Soak it in the sun
And make the strawberry-lemon pie?

26
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:07,040
CHILDREN: The Candy Man?
BILL: The Candy Man.

27
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:10,260
The Candy Man can.

28
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:14,210
[Singing] The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love

29
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,890
And makes the world taste good

30
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:19,800
Willy Wonka makes

31
00:04:19,890 --> 00:04:21,390
Everything he bakes

32
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:24,940
Satisfying and delicious

33
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:27,860
Talk about your childhood wishes

34
00:04:28,270 --> 00:04:31,610
You can even eat the dishes

35
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:37,320
[Lively instrumental music]

36
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:44,450
[Singing] Who can take tomorrow

37
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:46,710
Dip it in a dream

38
00:04:47,410 --> 00:04:50,830
Separate the sorrow
and collect up all the cream?

39
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:53,920
BILL: The Candy Man
CHILDREN: Willy Wonka can

40
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:56,800
The Candy Man can

41
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:02,220
The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love

42
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:06,010
And makes the world taste good

43
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:09,810
And the world tastes good

44
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:16,110
'Cause the Candy Man thinks it should

45
00:05:35,670 --> 00:05:37,090
CHARLIE: Hi, Mr. Jopeck.

46
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:39,840
MR. JOPECK: Come along, Charlie.
You're late.

47
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:44,470
CHARLIE: It's payday, Mr. Jopeck.
MR. JOPECK: You're right.

48
00:05:45,510 --> 00:05:47,850
MR. JOPECK: There you are.
CHARLIE: Thanks.

49
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:49,730
Say hello to your Grandpa Joe.

50
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:50,940
CHARLIE: Okay.

51
00:05:53,150 --> 00:05:55,570
[Fast-paced instrumental music]

52
00:06:06,410 --> 00:06:08,620
[Eerie instrumental music]

53
00:06:33,890 --> 00:06:36,020
THE TINKER: " Up the airy mountain

54
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:38,480
" Down the rushing glen

55
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:40,860
" We dare not go a-hunting

56
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:43,950
" For fear of little men"

57
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:48,700
You see, nobody ever goes in...

58
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:54,200
and nobody ever comes out.

59
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:06,090
Charlie's late.

60
00:07:06,550 --> 00:07:09,220
He works too hard for a little boy.

61
00:07:09,430 --> 00:07:11,430
GRANDPA: He should have
some time to play.

62
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:13,190
Not enough hours in the day.

63
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:16,400
MRS. BUCKET: With the four of you
bedridden for the past 20 years...

64
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,980
it takes a lot of work
to keep this family going.

65
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:21,350
If only his father were alive.

66
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:25,690
As soon as I get my strength back,
I'll get out of this bed and help him.

67
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:29,240
In all the years you've been saying
you'll get out of that bed...

68
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:32,160
I've yet to see you set foot on the floor.

69
00:07:33,250 --> 00:07:35,920
Maybe if the floor wasn't so cold...

70
00:07:37,250 --> 00:07:38,710
Hi, everybody.

71
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:42,090
Wake up, Charlie's home.

72
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:44,960
CHARLIE: Grandpa George.

73
00:07:46,430 --> 00:07:48,100
CHARLIE: Grandma Georgina.

74
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:50,180
CHARLIE: Grandma Josephine.

75
00:07:51,310 --> 00:07:53,150
CHARLIE: Grandpa Joe.

76
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:57,850
Is this your supper, Grandpa?

77
00:07:57,940 --> 00:07:59,690
It's yours, too, Charlie.

78
00:07:59,770 --> 00:08:02,730
I'm fed up with cabbage water.
It's not enough.

79
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:05,410
JOSEPHINE: It's all we have.
GRANDPA: What are you saying?

80
00:08:06,650 --> 00:08:09,530
CHARLIE: How about this?
MRS. BUCKET: Where did you get that?

81
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:13,210
What difference does it make
where he got it? Point is, he got it.

82
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:17,460
- It's my first payday.
- Good for you. We'll have a real banquet.

83
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:21,660
CHARLIE: Mom, here's what's left.
You keep it.

84
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:24,470
Except for this.

85
00:08:24,670 --> 00:08:27,760
From now on,
I'm going to pay for your tobacco.

86
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:31,980
No one's going to pay for it.
I'm giving it up.

87
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:34,720
Come on, Dad, it's only one pipe a day.

88
00:08:34,810 --> 00:08:38,440
When a loaf of bread looks like a banquet,
I've no right buying tobacco.

89
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:41,690
Go on, Grandpa. Please take it.

90
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:53,000
CHARLIE: After I finished my paper route,
I was in front of Wonka's.

91
00:08:53,490 --> 00:08:57,540
There was this strange man there.
I think he was a tinker.

92
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:01,460
He was standing right behind me
looking up at the factory.

93
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:03,800
Just before he left, he said.:

94
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,300
" Nobody ever goes in,
and nobody ever comes out. "

95
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:09,470
And right he was, Charlie.

96
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:12,470
Not since the tragic day
that Willy Wonka locked it.

97
00:09:12,550 --> 00:09:13,800
CHARLIE: Why did he lock it?

98
00:09:13,890 --> 00:09:17,310
Because all the other chocolate makers
were sending in spies...

99
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:20,850
dressed as workers
to steal Mr. Wonka's secret recipes.

100
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:25,020
Especially Slugworth.
That Slugworth was the worst.

101
00:09:25,570 --> 00:09:29,820
Finally, Mr. Wonka shouted,
" I shall be ruined! Close the factory. "

102
00:09:29,950 --> 00:09:32,450
And that's just what he did.

103
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:36,080
He locked the gates
and vanished completely.

104
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:39,330
And then suddenly,
about three years later...

105
00:09:39,410 --> 00:09:42,000
the most amazing thing happened.

106
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:44,960
The factory started working again,
full blast.

107
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:48,500
And more delicious candies
were coming out than ever before.

108
00:09:48,590 --> 00:09:51,720
But the gates stayed locked,
so that no one...

109
00:09:51,930 --> 00:09:54,810
not even Mr. Slugworth, could steal them.

110
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:59,020
But, Grandpa, someone must be helping
Mr. Wonka work the factory.

111
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:00,890
GRANDPA: Thousands
must be helping him.

112
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:03,480
But who? Who are they?

113
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,690
That is the biggest mystery of them all.

114
00:10:09,650 --> 00:10:11,740
TURKENTINE: Charlie Bucket?
CHARLIE: Yes, sir?

115
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:15,030
TURKENTINE: I shall need an assistant.
Come and give me a hand.

116
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:19,330
We have here nitric acid, glycerin...

117
00:10:19,410 --> 00:10:21,500
and a special mixture of my own.

118
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:24,580
Together, it's horrible, dangerous stuff.
Blows you up.

119
00:10:24,670 --> 00:10:28,090
But mixed together right, as only
I know how, what do you think it makes?

120
00:10:28,170 --> 00:10:30,340
CHARLIE: I don't know.
TURKENTINE: Of course not...

121
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:32,010
because only I know.

122
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:35,760
If you knew, you'd be teaching me
instead of me teaching you.

123
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:40,600
And for a student to teach his teacher
is presumptuous and rude. Is that clear?

124
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:42,020
CHARLIE: Yes, sir.
TURKENTINE: Good.

125
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:46,810
Mixed together in the right way,
these three highly dangerous ingredients...

126
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:49,280
make the finest wart remover
in the world.

127
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:52,110
TURKENTINE: The trick is to pour them in
in equal amounts.

128
00:10:52,190 --> 00:10:55,440
Charlie, you take the nitric acid
and the glycerin...

129
00:10:55,570 --> 00:10:58,740
and I'll take my own special mixture.
Are you ready?

130
00:10:58,830 --> 00:11:00,870
Good lad. Pour.

131
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:02,880
[Students laughing and clapping]

132
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:04,500
Did we do it wrong?

133
00:11:04,790 --> 00:11:07,920
TURKENTINE: No, certainly not.
This is for very big warts.

134
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:10,550
What on earth's going on out there?

135
00:11:10,630 --> 00:11:12,550
STUDENT 1: I hope there's still some left.

136
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:15,220
Winkelmann, come here.
What's happening?

137
00:11:15,300 --> 00:11:16,760
Willy Wonka's opening his factory.

138
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:19,010
- He's gonna let people in.
- Are you sure?

139
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:21,560
It's on the radio.
And he's giving chocolates away.

140
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,680
Class dismissed.

141
00:11:22,770 --> 00:11:24,520
WINKELMANN: No, it's only for five people.

142
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:26,270
Class un-dismissed.

143
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:29,900
He's hidden five Golden Tickets.
Whoever finds them wins the prize.

144
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:32,110
- Where's he hidden them?
- In five Wonka Bars.

145
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:35,780
- You've gotta buy them to find them.
- Class re-dismissed!

146
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:41,290
STUDENT 2: I'm going to buy
the whole store.

147
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:49,040
NEWSCASTER: Now, details
on the sudden announcement...

148
00:11:49,130 --> 00:11:51,760
that has captured the attention
of the entire world.

149
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,720
Hidden among the countless billions
of Wonka Bars...

150
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:56,720
are five Gold Tickets.

151
00:11:56,800 --> 00:12:01,180
And to the five people who find them
will come the most fabulous prize:

152
00:12:01,350 --> 00:12:03,770
A lifetime supply of chocolate.

153
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:05,640
As if this were not enough...

154
00:12:05,730 --> 00:12:09,730
each winner, before receiving his prize,
will be personally escorted...

155
00:12:09,810 --> 00:12:11,440
They're all crazy.

156
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:14,860
GRANDPA: The man's a genius.
He'll sell a million bars.

157
00:12:15,690 --> 00:12:18,650
Grandpa, do you think I've got a chance
to find one?

158
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:21,580
One? I'm counting on you to find all five.

159
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,040
One's enough for me.

160
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:26,370
NEWSCASTER: We have reports coming in
that the response is phenomenal.

161
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:29,510
Wonka Bars are disappearing
from candy store shelves...

162
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:31,580
at a rate to boggle the mind.

163
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:33,260
Truly, it is incredible...

164
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:36,260
the way that Wonkamania
has descended upon the globe.

165
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:39,470
While the world searches,
we watch and wait...

166
00:12:39,550 --> 00:12:41,680
wondering where the pursuit will lead...

167
00:12:41,760 --> 00:12:45,100
and how long the spirit of man will hold up
under the strain.

168
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:49,430
I'm still having these dreams, Doctor,
and I still can't stop believing them.

169
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:51,480
I've told you, Mr. Hoffstedder...

170
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:54,860
to believe in one's dreams
is a manifestation of insanity.

171
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:58,070
The sooner you accept this,
the sooner you'll get well.

172
00:12:58,150 --> 00:13:01,280
But I dreamed the archangel appeared
and whispered into my ear...

173
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:03,620
and told me where to find
a Golden Wonka Ticket.

174
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:05,290
What exactly did he say?

175
00:13:05,370 --> 00:13:08,750
What difference does that make?
This was a dream, a fantasy.

176
00:13:08,830 --> 00:13:11,750
HOFFSTEDDER: You said-
DOCTOR: Just tell me where the ticket is!

177
00:13:12,170 --> 00:13:14,130
We began with five Golden Tickets.

178
00:13:14,210 --> 00:13:18,970
Like five lucky bolts of lightning
ready to strike at any point on the map.

179
00:13:19,050 --> 00:13:22,010
No one knew where or when
the first one would hit.

180
00:13:22,090 --> 00:13:24,970
But as you all know,
last night we got our answer.

181
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:28,400
STANLEY: While we in America slept,
the first Golden Ticket was found...

182
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:31,480
in the small town of Duselheim, Germany.

183
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:34,480
We've been waiting several hours
for the follow-up story.

184
00:13:34,570 --> 00:13:36,280
We're finally ready with a live report.

185
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:39,900
Proud we are,
for the attention of the entire world...

186
00:13:39,990 --> 00:13:42,200
focuses today right here in Duselheim.

187
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:44,870
A community suddenly thrust
into prominence...

188
00:13:44,950 --> 00:13:48,870
by the unexpected discovery
of the first Wonka Golden Ticket.

189
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:52,130
Its lucky finder is the son
of a most prominent butcher.

190
00:13:52,210 --> 00:13:54,920
The boy's name, Augustus Gloop.

191
00:13:56,550 --> 00:14:00,220
The pride of Duselheim,
the fame of Western Germany.

192
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:03,220
An example for the whole world.

193
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:06,140
REPORTER 2: How do you feel
to be the first Golden Ticket finder?

194
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:08,220
AUGUSTUS: Hungry.
REPORTER 2: Any other feelings?

195
00:14:08,310 --> 00:14:11,690
I feel sorry for Wonka.
It's gonna cost him a fortune in fudge.

196
00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:13,940
REPORTER 1: Mr. Gloop,
would you mind saying-

197
00:14:14,350 --> 00:14:18,190
REPORTER 2: Would you care to say
a few words to the television audience?

198
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:22,160
I just knew Augustus would find
a Golden Ticket. Eating is his hobby.

199
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:23,330
MRS. GLOOP: We encourage him.

200
00:14:23,410 --> 00:14:26,460
He wouldn't do it
unless he needed the nourishment.

201
00:14:26,530 --> 00:14:28,700
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

202
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:31,080
ALL: Happy birthday, Charlie!

203
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,590
MRS. BUCKET: Here you are, Charlie.
CHARLIE: Thank you.

204
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:43,760
It's terrific.

205
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,220
We each knitted a bit: Grandma Georgina,
Grandma Josephine, and me.

206
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:49,930
I did the end pieces with the little tassels.

207
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:53,360
And here's a little gift
from Grandpa George and me.

208
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:55,860
I think I know what this is.

209
00:14:56,860 --> 00:15:01,110
- It is. A Wonka.
- Open it. Let's see that Golden Ticket.

210
00:15:01,190 --> 00:15:03,940
- Wouldn't that be fantastic?
- Don't raise his hopes.

211
00:15:04,030 --> 00:15:07,120
Never mind. Go on, open it.
I want to see that gold.

212
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,410
MRS. BUCKET: Stop it, Dad.

213
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:10,410
I've got the same chance as anybody else.

214
00:15:10,490 --> 00:15:11,950
GRANDPA: You've got more...

215
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:13,750
because you want it more.

216
00:15:13,830 --> 00:15:16,500
- Go on, open it.
- Here goes.

217
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:21,590
- I got it!
- Where?

218
00:15:21,670 --> 00:15:22,920
Let's see!

219
00:15:23,010 --> 00:15:26,470
Fooled you, didn't I?
You thought I really had it.

220
00:15:28,430 --> 00:15:31,680
Never mind, Charlie. You'll find one.

221
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:36,020
- Here, everybody have a bite.
- No.

222
00:15:47,910 --> 00:15:50,910
VERUCA: I wanted to be the first
to find a Golden Ticket, Daddy.

223
00:15:50,990 --> 00:15:54,240
MR. SALT: I know, angel.
We're doing the best we can.

224
00:15:54,330 --> 00:15:57,330
I've got every girl on the bleeding staff
hunting for you.

225
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:59,670
Then where is it?
Why haven't they found it?

226
00:15:59,750 --> 00:16:04,670
MR. SALT: Veruca, sweetheart,
I'm not a magician. Give me time!

227
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:08,010
I want it now!
What's the matter with those twerps?

228
00:16:08,090 --> 00:16:11,680
For five days now,
the entire factory's been on the job!

229
00:16:11,760 --> 00:16:14,600
They haven't shelled a peanut in there
since Monday!

230
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:18,100
They've been shelling
flaming chocolate bars from dawn to dusk!

231
00:16:18,190 --> 00:16:19,770
Make them work nights.

232
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:23,440
MR. SALT: Come along, you girls!

233
00:16:23,530 --> 00:16:26,830
MR. SALT: Put a jerk in it, or you'll be out
on your ears, all of you!

234
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:30,150
And listen to this,
the first girl that finds a Golden Ticket...

235
00:16:33,410 --> 00:16:35,200
What do you think of that?

236
00:16:35,290 --> 00:16:36,830
[Girls cheering]

237
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:38,410
VERUCA: They're not even trying.

238
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:41,340
They don't want to find it.
They're jealous of me.

239
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:43,670
Sweetheart, I can't push them no harder.

240
00:16:43,750 --> 00:16:46,170
19,000 bars an hour they're shelling.

241
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:50,010
MR. SALT: 760,000 they've done so far.
VERUCA: You promised, Daddy.

242
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:52,720
You promised I'd have it the very first day!

243
00:16:53,010 --> 00:16:57,060
You're going to be very unpopular, Henry,
if you don't deliver soon.

244
00:16:57,140 --> 00:17:00,520
It breaks my heart, Henrietta.
I hate to see her unhappy.

245
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:02,270
I won't talk to you ever again.

246
00:17:02,360 --> 00:17:05,780
You're a rotten, mean father!
You never give me anything I want!

247
00:17:05,860 --> 00:17:10,490
- I won't go to school till I have it.
- Veruca, sweetheart, angel.

248
00:17:10,570 --> 00:17:13,320
There are only four tickets left
in the whole world...

249
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:17,500
and the whole world's hunting for them!
What can I do?

250
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:20,670
I've got it, Mr. Salt! Here it is!

251
00:17:22,790 --> 00:17:25,380
VERUCA: About time, too! I want it!

252
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:27,050
[Cheering]

253
00:17:30,630 --> 00:17:33,590
VERUCA: Give me that ticket! It's mine!

254
00:17:33,800 --> 00:17:36,300
I found a Golden Ticket!

255
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:38,690
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

256
00:17:41,810 --> 00:17:43,140
Thank God.

257
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:48,320
Happiness is what counts with children.
Happiness and harmony.

258
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:53,990
REPORTER: This is the sign of our times.
The symbol of the havoc...

259
00:17:54,070 --> 00:17:56,910
the mad craze
that's sweeping the world today.

260
00:17:56,990 --> 00:18:01,240
Whatever corner of the globe we are in,
whichever continent we're on...

261
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:05,210
the great search for Wonka Bars
continues. We're nearing the end...

262
00:18:05,290 --> 00:18:07,460
of our 43rd day
in the hunt for Golden Tickets.

263
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:11,720
And everywhere,
we're beginning to see signs of anxiety.

264
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:13,340
Every hour on the hour...

265
00:18:13,430 --> 00:18:16,350
new shipments are being sent
to points around the globe.

266
00:18:16,430 --> 00:18:18,600
But they're just not moving fast enough.

267
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:23,060
As time passes, the men who seek them
become more and more desperate.

268
00:18:23,270 --> 00:18:26,020
COMPUTER EXPERT: Gentlemen,
I know how anxious you've been...

269
00:18:26,110 --> 00:18:27,530
during these last few days.

270
00:18:27,610 --> 00:18:29,990
But now I think I can safely say...

271
00:18:30,190 --> 00:18:33,070
that your time and money
have been well-spent.

272
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:36,950
We're about to witness
the greatest miracle of the Machine Age.

273
00:18:37,160 --> 00:18:40,160
Based on the revolutionary
law of probability...

274
00:18:40,250 --> 00:18:43,880
this machine will tell us
the precise location...

275
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:47,090
of the three remaining Golden Tickets.

276
00:18:56,010 --> 00:19:00,060
It says,
" I won't tell. That would be cheating. "

277
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:08,730
I am now telling the computer...

278
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:13,030
that if it will tell me the correct answer,
I will gladly share with it...

279
00:19:13,110 --> 00:19:14,860
the grand prize.

280
00:19:19,330 --> 00:19:20,460
He says:

281
00:19:20,540 --> 00:19:24,630
" What would a computer do
with a lifetime supply of chocolate?"

282
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:26,370
[Laughing sheepishly]

283
00:19:29,420 --> 00:19:33,970
I am now telling the computer
exactly what he can do with the chocolate.

284
00:19:36,760 --> 00:19:40,100
TV ANNOUNCER: It can happen
right here, too, unbelievable as it sounds...

285
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:41,810
right here in America.

286
00:19:41,890 --> 00:19:46,730
Where even in the smallest town,
the happiest of dreams can come true.

287
00:19:46,810 --> 00:19:48,440
Because, folks, here she is.:

288
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:52,230
Miss Violet Beauregarde,
finder of Golden Ticket number three.

289
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:54,030
From Miles City, Montana.

290
00:19:54,110 --> 00:19:55,780
And with her, the proud parents.

291
00:19:55,860 --> 00:19:58,990
Mr. Beauregarde,
a prominent local politician, a civic leader.

292
00:19:59,070 --> 00:20:01,530
Sam Beauregarde,
'"Square Deal'" Sam to you...

293
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:03,710
with today's great giveaway bargains.

294
00:20:03,790 --> 00:20:06,290
The finest values you'll get
in the entire country.

295
00:20:06,370 --> 00:20:08,620
SAM: This number here-
VIOLET: They don't want you!

296
00:20:08,710 --> 00:20:10,710
- Care to say a few words?
- Sure I will.

297
00:20:10,790 --> 00:20:13,250
Here is Golden Ticket number three,
and it's all mine.

298
00:20:13,340 --> 00:20:14,840
TV ANNOUNCER: Tell us how it happened.

299
00:20:14,920 --> 00:20:17,840
I'm a gum chewer,
but when I heard about these tickets...

300
00:20:17,970 --> 00:20:20,890
I laid off gum
and switched to candy bars instead.

301
00:20:20,970 --> 00:20:23,060
Now, of course, I'm right back on gum.

302
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:26,230
I chew all day, except at meals,
when I stick it behind my ear.

303
00:20:26,310 --> 00:20:27,940
MOTHER: Violet.
VIOLET: Cool it, Mother.

304
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:32,770
This is a piece of gum I've been chewing
on for three months solid. A world record.

305
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:36,200
It's beaten the record
held by my best friend, Cornelia...

306
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:38,120
and was she mad.

307
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,990
Cornelia, how are you, sweetie?

308
00:20:40,070 --> 00:20:43,160
Let me just butt in to say
if any of you folks watching...

309
00:20:43,290 --> 00:20:45,960
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

310
00:20:51,630 --> 00:20:52,960
[Doorbell rings]

311
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:55,590
Charlie, what are you doing here?

312
00:20:55,670 --> 00:20:58,010
I thought if you were ready,
I'd walk you home.

313
00:20:58,090 --> 00:21:01,340
I wish I were. Looks like
I'm going to be here late tonight.

314
00:21:01,430 --> 00:21:05,640
CHARLIE: Then I guess I'll be going.
MRS. BUCKET: Stay for a minute.

315
00:21:05,890 --> 00:21:08,980
Here. Pull up a pile of clothes
and sit down.

316
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:13,350
Everything all right at school?

317
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:15,230
CHARLIE: Yep.
MRS. BUCKET: Good.

318
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,280
Go on your newspaper route today?

319
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:20,610
CHARLIE: Just finished.
MRS. BUCKET: Good.

320
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:25,370
I wanted to tell you something.

321
00:21:28,210 --> 00:21:31,380
CHARLIE: They found the third ticket today.
MRS. BUCKET: Did they?

322
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:36,880
I guess I'll be going now.

323
00:21:38,130 --> 00:21:39,720
Is that all?

324
00:21:40,550 --> 00:21:42,510
I thought you'd like to know.

325
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:46,270
Most people are pretty interested.
I know I'm interested.

326
00:21:46,470 --> 00:21:49,810
There are only two tickets left, you know.
Just two.

327
00:21:49,890 --> 00:21:51,600
CHARLIE: Pretty soon, just one.

328
00:21:51,690 --> 00:21:53,730
I wonder who the lucky ones will be.

329
00:21:53,810 --> 00:21:56,150
In case you're wondering, it won't be me.

330
00:21:56,230 --> 00:21:59,360
Just in case you're wondering,
you can count me out.

331
00:22:00,200 --> 00:22:02,660
There are 100 billion people in this world...

332
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:05,790
and only five will find Golden Tickets.

333
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:09,420
Even if you had a sackful of money,
you probably wouldn't find one.

334
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:14,260
After this is over, you'll be no different
from billions of others who didn't find one.

335
00:22:14,330 --> 00:22:17,380
But I am different.
I want it more than any of them.

336
00:22:18,300 --> 00:22:21,550
You'll get your chance.
One day things will change.

337
00:22:21,760 --> 00:22:24,100
When? When will they change?

338
00:22:24,340 --> 00:22:26,680
Probably when you least expect it.

339
00:22:29,930 --> 00:22:31,180
See you later.

340
00:22:38,530 --> 00:22:40,910
[Melancholic instrumental music]

341
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:49,120
[Singing] You get blue like everyone

342
00:22:49,700 --> 00:22:53,000
But me and Grandpa Joe

343
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:56,920
Can make your troubles go away

344
00:22:57,750 --> 00:22:59,790
Blow away

345
00:23:00,960 --> 00:23:04,170
There they go

346
00:23:05,550 --> 00:23:07,800
Cheer up, Charlie

347
00:23:09,010 --> 00:23:11,350
Give me a smile

348
00:23:12,060 --> 00:23:16,940
What happened to that smile
I used to know?

349
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:20,980
Don't you know your grin

350
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:24,820
Has always been my sunshine

351
00:23:25,700 --> 00:23:30,580
Let that sunshine show

352
00:23:31,870 --> 00:23:34,080
Come on, Charlie

353
00:23:35,670 --> 00:23:38,550
No need to frown

354
00:23:38,840 --> 00:23:40,510
Deep down you know

355
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:44,720
The world is still your toy

356
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:48,430
When the world gets heavy

357
00:23:48,510 --> 00:23:51,600
Never pit-a-pat 'em

358
00:23:52,180 --> 00:23:56,640
Up and at 'em, boy

359
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:03,320
Someday, sweet as a song

360
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:08,490
Charlie's lucky day will come along

361
00:24:10,450 --> 00:24:15,960
Till that day
You've got to stay strong, Charlie

362
00:24:17,210 --> 00:24:22,840
Up on top is right where you belong

363
00:24:24,380 --> 00:24:27,720
Look up, Charlie

364
00:24:28,140 --> 00:24:31,100
You'll see a star

365
00:24:31,310 --> 00:24:36,480
Just follow it
And keep your dream in view

366
00:24:38,650 --> 00:24:44,530
Pretty soon the sky
is gonna clear up, Charlie

367
00:24:45,900 --> 00:24:49,900
Cheer up, Charlie, do

368
00:24:52,490 --> 00:24:55,160
Cheer up, Charlie

369
00:24:57,120 --> 00:25:03,250
Just be glad you're you

370
00:25:09,130 --> 00:25:10,010
[Gunshots on TV]

371
00:25:10,090 --> 00:25:12,260
NEWSCASTER: While the rest of the world
searches...

372
00:25:12,350 --> 00:25:14,520
here in the Southwest,
it has actually happened.

373
00:25:14,600 --> 00:25:18,690
That's what I said. There's only
one Golden Ticket left in the entire world.

374
00:25:18,770 --> 00:25:22,440
Because right here, in our own community
of Marble Falls, Arizona...

375
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:24,440
is lucky winner number four.

376
00:25:24,530 --> 00:25:28,490
The name soon to be heard
around the universe is Mr. Mike Teevee.

377
00:25:28,570 --> 00:25:30,280
Can we shut that thing off?

378
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:31,570
Are you crazy?

379
00:25:31,660 --> 00:25:33,290
MRS. TEEVEE: He'll answer
during the break.

380
00:25:33,370 --> 00:25:35,660
NEWSCASTER: The country
wants to hear from you.

381
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:37,870
Can't you shut up? I'm busy.

382
00:25:37,960 --> 00:25:39,210
What a great show.

383
00:25:39,290 --> 00:25:42,920
I serve all his TV dinners right here.
He's never even been to the table.

384
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:44,880
- You love to watch TV?
- You bet.

385
00:25:44,960 --> 00:25:48,300
- What about that Golden Ticket-
- Hold it. I want to catch this.

386
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:49,630
REPORTER 1: Like the killings?

387
00:25:49,720 --> 00:25:51,470
What do you think life's all about?

388
00:25:51,550 --> 00:25:53,800
REPORTER 2: Mike, would you tell us if-

389
00:25:54,970 --> 00:25:57,890
Wait till I get a real one. Colt.45.

390
00:25:57,980 --> 00:26:02,230
- Pop won't let me have one yet, will you?
- Not till you're 12, Son.

391
00:26:04,770 --> 00:26:07,150
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

392
00:26:08,900 --> 00:26:10,780
Four down, one to go.

393
00:26:10,990 --> 00:26:15,120
And somewhere out there,
another lucky person is moving closer...

394
00:26:15,330 --> 00:26:19,790
to finding the last
of the most sought-after prizes in history.

395
00:26:20,410 --> 00:26:23,330
Though we cannot help but envy him,
whoever he is...

396
00:26:23,420 --> 00:26:26,510
and we might be tempted
to be bitter at our losing...

397
00:26:26,590 --> 00:26:29,510
we must remember
there are many more important things.

398
00:26:29,590 --> 00:26:32,090
Many more important things.

399
00:26:32,590 --> 00:26:36,510
Offhand, I can't think of what they are,
but I'm sure there must be something.

400
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,100
Now for tomorrow's weather and...

401
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:43,440
CHARLIE: Why did you wake me up,
Grandpa? Is something wrong?

402
00:26:49,610 --> 00:26:51,950
Grandpa, that money was for tobacco.

403
00:26:52,070 --> 00:26:55,740
I told you, I've given it up. Go on, open it.

404
00:26:55,950 --> 00:27:00,000
- One ticket left. Let's see that gold.
- No, you do it. I can't.

405
00:27:01,250 --> 00:27:03,960
Something tells me
we're going to be lucky this time.

406
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:06,460
I've got a funny feeling inside.

407
00:27:08,300 --> 00:27:10,640
Which end shall I open first?

408
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:14,010
That end. Just a tiny bit.

409
00:27:15,640 --> 00:27:18,230
- Like this?
- Now a bit more.

410
00:27:19,390 --> 00:27:22,390
- You finish it. I can't.
- No, Grandpa. You do it.

411
00:27:22,810 --> 00:27:25,560
All right. Here goes.

412
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:33,660
You know...

413
00:27:34,570 --> 00:27:38,570
I bet those Golden Tickets
make the chocolate taste terrible.

414
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:46,460
[People chattering]

415
00:27:49,290 --> 00:27:50,920
AUCTIONEER: Lot 403.

416
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,550
I can personally guarantee,
ladies and gentlemen...

417
00:27:53,630 --> 00:27:57,430
that this is the one and only,
the absolutely last case of Wonka Bars...

418
00:27:57,510 --> 00:27:59,300
left in the United Kingdom.

419
00:28:13,280 --> 00:28:14,950
Your Majesty.

420
00:28:18,450 --> 00:28:20,120
DETECTIVE: I'm sorry, Mrs. Curtis.

421
00:28:20,200 --> 00:28:22,700
There's nothing in these papers
to give us a clue.

422
00:28:22,790 --> 00:28:24,960
They kidnapped my husband 12 hours ago.

423
00:28:25,040 --> 00:28:27,330
When will we hear from them?
What do they want?

424
00:28:27,420 --> 00:28:31,380
Try to stay calm. They did it for ransom.
We must wait to hear their demands.

425
00:28:31,460 --> 00:28:33,800
I'll give them anything they want.

426
00:28:33,880 --> 00:28:35,970
All I want is to have Harold back.

427
00:28:36,050 --> 00:28:37,590
[Phone ringing]

428
00:28:41,140 --> 00:28:42,930
DETECTIVE: Go ahead, we're listening.

429
00:28:48,230 --> 00:28:51,650
What did they asked for?
Whatever it is, they can have it.

430
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:54,230
They want your case of Wonka Bars.

431
00:28:59,660 --> 00:29:01,330
Did you hear me?

432
00:29:01,990 --> 00:29:05,410
It's your husband's life
or your case of Wonka Bars.

433
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:08,500
How long will they give me
to think it over?

434
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:13,750
That's it, it's all over.
The Wonka contest is all over.

435
00:29:13,840 --> 00:29:16,010
The fifth and final ticket has been found.

436
00:29:16,090 --> 00:29:20,600
We've got a live report coming in
directly from Paraguay, South America.

437
00:29:20,680 --> 00:29:23,270
Ladies and gentlemen, it is finished.
The end has come.

438
00:29:23,350 --> 00:29:26,190
The fifth and last Golden Ticket
has just been found...

439
00:29:26,270 --> 00:29:27,770
right here in Paraguay.

440
00:29:27,850 --> 00:29:30,940
The finder is lucky Alberto Minoleta...

441
00:29:31,020 --> 00:29:34,940
the multimillionaire owner of gambling
casinos throughout South America.

442
00:29:35,030 --> 00:29:38,490
Here is the most recent picture available
of the happy finder.

443
00:29:38,570 --> 00:29:41,280
- The man who has finally...
- Turn it off.

444
00:29:42,700 --> 00:29:44,660
Well, that's that.

445
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:48,790
- No more Golden Tickets.
- A lot of rubbish, the whole thing.

446
00:29:48,870 --> 00:29:50,460
Not to Charlie, it wasn't.

447
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:55,340
A little boy needs something to hope for.
What's he got to hope for now?

448
00:29:55,550 --> 00:29:57,640
GEORGINA: Who's going to tell him?

449
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:00,890
MRS. BUCKET: Let's not wake him.
He'll find out soon enough.

450
00:30:00,970 --> 00:30:04,640
GRANDPA: Yeah, let him sleep.
Let him have one last dream.

451
00:30:06,220 --> 00:30:07,810
[Slow instrumental music]

452
00:30:10,770 --> 00:30:12,610
[Turkentine clearing throat]

453
00:30:12,810 --> 00:30:15,730
I've just decided to switch
our Friday's schedule to Monday.

454
00:30:15,820 --> 00:30:19,410
Which means Friday's test
on what we learned during the week...

455
00:30:19,490 --> 00:30:22,080
will now take place on Monday
before we've learned it.

456
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:24,910
Since today's Tuesday,
it doesn't matter in the slightest.

457
00:30:24,990 --> 00:30:26,740
TURKENTINE: Pencils ready.

458
00:30:28,160 --> 00:30:31,080
Today we are going to learn about...

459
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:33,670
percentages.

460
00:30:33,750 --> 00:30:37,380
For example, let's take
the recent unpleasantness.

461
00:30:37,590 --> 00:30:40,800
Supposing that there were
1,000 Wonka Bars in the world...

462
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:44,680
and during the contest you each opened
a certain number of them.

463
00:30:44,850 --> 00:30:47,020
That number is...

464
00:30:47,560 --> 00:30:49,730
a percent. Everyone understand?

465
00:30:49,810 --> 00:30:51,350
[Students moaning]

466
00:30:51,440 --> 00:30:54,530
Madeline Durkin,
how many Wonka Bars did you open?

467
00:30:54,610 --> 00:30:55,860
About 100.

468
00:30:55,940 --> 00:31:00,740
There are ten 100s in 1,000.
Therefore, you opened 10%.

469
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:03,780
Peter Goff, how many did you open?

470
00:31:03,860 --> 00:31:05,450
One hundred and fifty.

471
00:31:05,530 --> 00:31:09,280
That's 10% half over again,
which makes 15%.

472
00:31:09,540 --> 00:31:12,840
- Charlie Bucket, how many did you open?
- Two.

473
00:31:12,960 --> 00:31:17,170
- That's easy. 200 is twice-
- Not 200.

474
00:31:17,710 --> 00:31:19,170
Just two.

475
00:31:19,380 --> 00:31:22,470
Two? What do you mean,
you only opened two?

476
00:31:22,630 --> 00:31:24,720
I don't care very much for chocolate.

477
00:31:24,800 --> 00:31:29,100
I can't figure out just two.
So let's pretend you opened 200.

478
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:33,520
If you opened 200 Wonka Bars,
apart from being dreadfully sick...

479
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:35,480
you'd have used up 20% of 1,000...

480
00:31:35,560 --> 00:31:38,480
which is 15% half over again, 10%...

481
00:31:55,330 --> 00:31:58,120
[Mysterious instrumental music]

482
00:32:06,220 --> 00:32:08,760
[Cheerful instrumental music]

483
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:27,360
[Charlie clearing throat]

484
00:32:28,070 --> 00:32:31,200
CHARLIE: I'd like a bar of chocolate, please.
BILL: Sure.

485
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:32,790
What kind?

486
00:32:32,870 --> 00:32:35,960
A Slugworth Sizzler?
A Wonka Scrumdiddleumptious?

487
00:32:36,040 --> 00:32:39,670
- Whichever is biggest.
- Try the Wonka Scrumdiddleumptious.

488
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:44,180
Now that the tickets have been found,
I don't have to hide them anymore.

489
00:32:47,630 --> 00:32:49,220
[Bill clearing throat]

490
00:32:56,060 --> 00:32:57,560
Take it easy.

491
00:32:57,640 --> 00:33:00,310
You'll get a stomachache
if you swallow it like that.

492
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:02,280
- Bye.
- Bye, now.

493
00:33:10,070 --> 00:33:13,410
I think I'll buy just one more,
for my Grandpa Joe.

494
00:33:13,620 --> 00:33:14,910
Sure.

495
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:18,000
Why not try
a regular Wonka Bar this time?

496
00:33:18,080 --> 00:33:19,420
CHARLIE: Fine.

497
00:33:23,590 --> 00:33:25,180
[People talking excitedly]

498
00:33:27,510 --> 00:33:30,470
MR. JOPECK: Hear about the scandal.
MAN 1: Give me a newspaper.

499
00:33:30,550 --> 00:33:32,220
MR. JOPECK: All right.

500
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:35,640
MR. JOPECK: Take it easy. One at a time.
MAN 2: Did you hear the news?

501
00:33:35,720 --> 00:33:38,100
MAN 3: That gambler from Paraguay
made a phony ticket.

502
00:33:38,190 --> 00:33:41,440
MAN 2: That means there's
one Golden Ticket still floating around.

503
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:45,230
MAN 3: Can you imagine the nerve
of that guy? Trying to fool the whole world.

504
00:33:45,320 --> 00:33:49,160
MAN 2: He really was a crook.
This means the contest goes on forever.

505
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:04,500
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

506
00:34:23,980 --> 00:34:26,900
[Triumphant instrumental music]

507
00:34:32,410 --> 00:34:35,120
You've got it!
You've got the last Golden Ticket!

508
00:34:35,200 --> 00:34:38,040
The kid's found the last Golden Ticket!

509
00:34:39,450 --> 00:34:40,990
MAN 4: It really is gold.

510
00:34:41,080 --> 00:34:44,130
MR. JOPECK: Stand back. Leave him alone.
WOMAN: Let me see.

511
00:34:44,210 --> 00:34:46,250
MR. JOPECK: You'll kill him.
Leave him alone.

512
00:34:46,340 --> 00:34:48,840
MR. JOPECK: Break it up!
MAN 5: Show it over here.

513
00:34:48,920 --> 00:34:51,460
MR. JOPECK: Come on, Charlie.
Hold on to the ticket.

514
00:34:51,550 --> 00:34:55,970
MR. JOPECK: Run for it! Run straight home,
and don't stop till you get there!

515
00:34:56,140 --> 00:34:58,600
[Fast-paced instrumental music]

516
00:35:25,420 --> 00:35:27,710
[Ominous instrumental music]

517
00:35:27,920 --> 00:35:30,960
I congratulate you, little boy. Well done.

518
00:35:31,420 --> 00:35:33,880
You've found the fifth Golden Ticket.

519
00:35:34,090 --> 00:35:37,340
May I introduce myself? Arthur Slugworth.

520
00:35:37,930 --> 00:35:40,720
President of Slugworth Chocolates, Inc.

521
00:35:41,310 --> 00:35:45,230
Listen carefully,
because I'm going to make you very rich.

522
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:49,150
Mr. Wonka is at this moment
working on a fantastic invention.

523
00:35:49,650 --> 00:35:51,940
The Everlasting Gobstopper.

524
00:35:52,610 --> 00:35:54,740
If he succeeds, he'll ruin me.

525
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:58,240
So I want you to get hold of
just one Everlasting Gobstopper...

526
00:35:58,330 --> 00:36:01,920
and bring it to me
so that I can find the secret formula.

527
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:05,090
Your reward will be 10,000 of these.

528
00:36:05,790 --> 00:36:09,630
Think it over, will you?
A new house for your family...

529
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:13,130
and good food and comfort
for the rest of their lives.

530
00:36:13,970 --> 00:36:15,890
And don't forget the name:

531
00:36:15,970 --> 00:36:18,180
Everlasting Gobstopper.

532
00:36:27,810 --> 00:36:30,520
[Cheerful instrumental music]

533
00:36:47,620 --> 00:36:51,290
Look, everybody!
I've got it! The fifth Golden Ticket is mine!

534
00:36:51,380 --> 00:36:55,970
You're pulling our legs, Charlie.
There aren't any more Golden Tickets.

535
00:36:56,050 --> 00:36:59,300
No, Grandpa, the last one was a fake.
It said so in the papers.

536
00:36:59,390 --> 00:37:02,980
I found some money, bought a Wonka Bar,
and the ticket was in it!

537
00:37:03,060 --> 00:37:05,400
Look at it. See for yourself.

538
00:37:06,060 --> 00:37:08,520
Read it, Joe, for heaven's sake.

539
00:37:09,060 --> 00:37:13,360
" Greetings to you,
the lucky finder of this Golden Ticket...

540
00:37:13,570 --> 00:37:17,660
" from Mr. Willy Wonka.
Present this ticket at the factory gates...

541
00:37:17,740 --> 00:37:21,990
" at 10:00 in the morning on October 1,
and do not be late.

542
00:37:22,200 --> 00:37:27,000
" You may bring with you one member
of your own family, but no one else.

543
00:37:27,210 --> 00:37:30,340
" In your wildest dreams,
you could not imagine...

544
00:37:30,420 --> 00:37:33,130
" the marvelous surprises that await you. "

545
00:37:33,210 --> 00:37:36,340
GRANDPA: Charlie, you've done it!
MRS. BUCKET: I can't believe it.

546
00:37:37,220 --> 00:37:41,270
It says I can take somebody with me.
I wish you could go.

547
00:37:59,070 --> 00:38:00,150
[Grandpa groans]

548
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:03,410
That's good, Charlie. Now help me up.

549
00:38:17,260 --> 00:38:20,640
CHARLIE: Are you okay?
GRANDPA: I'm fine, Charlie.

550
00:38:25,850 --> 00:38:26,930
[Charlie exclaiming]

551
00:38:27,020 --> 00:38:28,020
MRS. BUCKET: Easy, Dad.
JOSEPHINE: Joe!

552
00:38:30,640 --> 00:38:32,140
JOSEPHINE: Watch it, Joe.

553
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:42,820
Look at me.

554
00:38:45,700 --> 00:38:47,290
Look at me!

555
00:38:48,330 --> 00:38:50,330
Up and about.

556
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:55,590
I haven't done this in 20 years.

557
00:38:56,380 --> 00:38:57,840
Grandpa.

558
00:39:01,800 --> 00:39:05,680
[Singing] I never thought my life could be

559
00:39:06,810 --> 00:39:10,690
Anything but catastrophe

560
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:15,150
But suddenly I begin to see

561
00:39:15,810 --> 00:39:20,060
A bit of good luck for me

562
00:39:20,820 --> 00:39:24,820
'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket

563
00:39:26,410 --> 00:39:32,540
I've got a golden twinkle in my eye

564
00:39:36,670 --> 00:39:39,550
I never had a chance to shine

565
00:39:39,630 --> 00:39:41,800
Never a happy song to sing

566
00:39:41,880 --> 00:39:43,920
But suddenly half the world is mine

567
00:39:44,010 --> 00:39:45,600
What an amazing thing!

568
00:39:45,800 --> 00:39:47,760
'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket

569
00:39:47,850 --> 00:39:49,640
It's ours, Charlie.

570
00:39:49,850 --> 00:39:53,600
[Singing] I've got a golden sun up in the sky

571
00:39:53,690 --> 00:39:55,360
Slippers, Charlie.

572
00:39:55,900 --> 00:39:57,650
[Lively instrumental music]

573
00:39:57,730 --> 00:40:01,320
[Singing] I never thought I'd see the day
When I would face the world and say

574
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:04,490
GRANDPA AND CHARLIE: '"Good morning,
look at the sun '"

575
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:08,620
GRANDPA: I never thought that I would be
Slap in the lap of luxury

576
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:10,490
Because I'd have said.:

577
00:40:10,580 --> 00:40:12,120
CHARLIE: '"It couldn't be done'"

578
00:40:12,330 --> 00:40:14,080
GRANDPA: But it can be done

579
00:40:15,670 --> 00:40:17,670
The cane, Charlie.

580
00:40:20,710 --> 00:40:22,340
[Grandpa laughing]

581
00:40:22,420 --> 00:40:24,010
GRANDPA: Here I go.

582
00:40:24,930 --> 00:40:26,770
[Laughing] Watch my speed.

583
00:40:32,060 --> 00:40:33,270
[Grandpa singing]

584
00:40:33,350 --> 00:40:37,020
I never dreamed that I would climb
Over the moon in ecstasy

585
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:40,730
But nevertheless it's there
That I'm shortly about to be

586
00:40:40,820 --> 00:40:43,030
GRANDPA AND CHARLIE:
'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket

587
00:40:44,360 --> 00:40:48,160
I've got a golden chance to make my way

588
00:40:48,370 --> 00:40:53,210
And with a Golden Ticket, it's a golden day

589
00:40:56,580 --> 00:40:58,830
[Lively instrumental music continues]

590
00:41:09,850 --> 00:41:12,850
Good morning! Look at the sun!

591
00:41:17,140 --> 00:41:20,890
GRANDPA AND CHARLIE: [Singing]
Because I'd have said it couldn't be done

592
00:41:21,230 --> 00:41:23,900
But it can be done

593
00:41:24,570 --> 00:41:28,570
I never dreamed that I would climb
Over the moon in ecstasy

594
00:41:28,660 --> 00:41:32,540
But nevertheless it's there
That I'm shortly about to be

595
00:41:32,740 --> 00:41:35,240
'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket

596
00:41:35,580 --> 00:41:37,580
GRANDPA AND CHARLIE:
I've got a Golden Ticket

597
00:41:37,660 --> 00:41:41,290
I've got a golden chance to make my way

598
00:41:41,500 --> 00:41:46,340
And with a Golden Ticket it's a golden day

599
00:41:50,340 --> 00:41:53,840
Stop! It says October 1. That's tomorrow.

600
00:41:53,930 --> 00:41:58,230
We've got a lot to do. Comb your hair,
wash your face, polish your shoes-

601
00:41:58,310 --> 00:42:00,150
MRS. BUCKET: I'll take care of everything.

602
00:42:00,230 --> 00:42:01,360
We don't have time.

603
00:42:01,440 --> 00:42:05,610
Grandpa, on the way home today,
I ran into Mr. Slugworth.

604
00:42:07,530 --> 00:42:10,030
[Marching band
instrumental music playing]

605
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:32,140
Hey, Mom, we're on TV.

606
00:42:32,220 --> 00:42:34,310
Hi, everybody in Marble Falls!

607
00:42:34,390 --> 00:42:38,230
Hi, Billy. Hi, Maggie. Hi, Fishface.
How do I look?

608
00:42:38,310 --> 00:42:39,900
ANNOUNCER: You guys ready?
MAN: You're on.

609
00:42:39,980 --> 00:42:41,770
This is the big day, folks.

610
00:42:41,850 --> 00:42:46,730
The day Willy Wonka will open his gates
and shower gifts on the five lucky winners.

611
00:42:46,820 --> 00:42:50,740
From everywhere, people have gathered
waiting for the hour to strike...

612
00:42:50,820 --> 00:42:54,490
waiting to catch a glimpse
of that legendary magician, Willy Wonka.

613
00:42:56,160 --> 00:42:59,790
Hi, Sam Beauregarde here. Don't forget
to visit Beauregarde's Auto Mart-

614
00:42:59,870 --> 00:43:02,830
Cut it out, Dad!
For heaven's sake, this is my show.

615
00:43:02,920 --> 00:43:06,760
Hi, Cornelia, sweetie. I've still got it.
How's this for a stretch?

616
00:43:11,470 --> 00:43:12,890
[People chattering]

617
00:43:19,930 --> 00:43:24,270
- I want to go in first before anybody else.
- Anything you say, sweetheart.

618
00:43:27,520 --> 00:43:30,940
Save some room for later,
Augustus, Liebling.

619
00:43:33,860 --> 00:43:37,700
[Whispering] Grandpa, I don't believe it.
We did it. We're actually going in.

620
00:43:37,780 --> 00:43:41,530
We're going to see
the greatest of them all, Mr. Willy Wonka.

621
00:43:41,790 --> 00:43:43,880
[Clock dinging]

622
00:44:08,650 --> 00:44:10,820
[People cheering and applauding]

623
00:45:17,590 --> 00:45:19,630
[People cheering]

624
00:45:22,890 --> 00:45:24,220
WONKA: Thank you.

625
00:45:28,810 --> 00:45:32,360
Welcome, my friends.
Welcome to my chocolate factory.

626
00:45:32,980 --> 00:45:35,190
Would you come forward, please?

627
00:45:35,400 --> 00:45:38,320
Get back, you.
Come on, Veruca, sweetheart.

628
00:45:39,320 --> 00:45:41,240
[Cheering]

629
00:45:43,450 --> 00:45:46,040
[Whispering] That's Slugworth,
the man I told you about.

630
00:45:50,540 --> 00:45:53,340
WONKA: Welcome.
It's nice to have you here.

631
00:45:53,420 --> 00:45:55,300
I'm so glad you could come.

632
00:45:55,380 --> 00:45:59,300
This is going to be such an exciting day.
I hope you enjoy it.

633
00:45:59,720 --> 00:46:01,220
I think you will.

634
00:46:01,430 --> 00:46:04,640
And now would you please show me
your Golden Tickets?

635
00:46:04,810 --> 00:46:06,310
I'm Veruca Salt.

636
00:46:06,390 --> 00:46:08,520
My dear Veruca, what a pleasure.

637
00:46:08,600 --> 00:46:12,770
- You look pretty in that lovely mink coat.
- I've got three others at home.

638
00:46:12,860 --> 00:46:17,200
Mr. Salt, overjoyed to see you, sir.
Would you just step over there?

639
00:46:17,400 --> 00:46:20,700
- Augustus Gloop.
- Augustus, my dear boy.

640
00:46:20,780 --> 00:46:23,450
How good to see you,
and in such fine shape.

641
00:46:23,580 --> 00:46:26,960
This must be the radiant Mrs. Gloop.
Just over there, dear lady.

642
00:46:27,040 --> 00:46:28,380
Violet Beauregarde.

643
00:46:28,460 --> 00:46:30,920
WONKA: Darling child.
Welcome to Wonka's.

644
00:46:31,000 --> 00:46:34,340
VIOLET: What kind of gum you got here?
WONKA: Charming.

645
00:46:34,420 --> 00:46:37,590
- Sam Beauregarde here.
- My dear sir, what a genuine pleasure.

646
00:46:37,670 --> 00:46:40,840
Any automotive needs, call on Sam.
Phone number's on the card.

647
00:46:40,930 --> 00:46:43,020
" With Sam B., it's a guarantee. "

648
00:46:44,100 --> 00:46:45,730
I'm Mike Teevee.

649
00:46:45,810 --> 00:46:49,020
MIKE: Wham, you're dead!
WONKA: Wonderful to meet you, Mike.

650
00:46:49,100 --> 00:46:50,890
WONKA: Mrs. Teevee, how do you do?

651
00:46:50,980 --> 00:46:53,070
- What an adorable boy you have.
- Thank you.

652
00:46:53,150 --> 00:46:55,440
- Just over there.
- Charlie Bucket.

653
00:46:55,520 --> 00:47:00,110
WONKA: I read about you in
the papers. I'm happy for you. Who's this?

654
00:47:00,200 --> 00:47:03,160
CHARLIE: My grandfather, Grandpa Joe.
WONKA: Delighted to meet you.

655
00:47:03,240 --> 00:47:05,280
Overjoyed. Enraptured. Entranced.

656
00:47:05,370 --> 00:47:07,960
Are we ready? Yes. Good. In we go.

657
00:47:08,450 --> 00:47:10,950
[Marching band instrumental music
resumes playing]

658
00:47:43,990 --> 00:47:46,330
Hats, coats, galoshes over here.

659
00:47:46,490 --> 00:47:49,660
But hurry, please.
We have so much time and so little to see.

660
00:47:49,750 --> 00:47:51,710
Wait a minute. Strike that.

661
00:47:51,910 --> 00:47:54,000
Reverse it. Thank you.

662
00:47:55,880 --> 00:47:59,590
VIOLET: When do I get my chocolate?
SAM: First take off your coat, Violet.

663
00:47:59,670 --> 00:48:02,260
MIKE: Boy, what weird-looking
coat hangers.

664
00:48:02,510 --> 00:48:03,590
[Violet and Mike scream]

665
00:48:03,680 --> 00:48:08,100
Little surprises around every corner,
but nothing dangerous. Don't be alarmed.

666
00:48:08,430 --> 00:48:12,140
As soon as your outer vestments
are in hand, we'll begin.

667
00:48:13,140 --> 00:48:17,310
Now, will the children kindly step up here.

668
00:48:28,370 --> 00:48:30,540
VIOLET: Accidents? What kind
of accidents?

669
00:48:30,620 --> 00:48:33,120
MRS. TEEVEE: I didn't know
we had to sign for this tour.

670
00:48:33,210 --> 00:48:35,340
VIOLET: I can't see what it says
in the bottom.

671
00:48:35,420 --> 00:48:37,300
Violet, you first.

672
00:48:37,540 --> 00:48:39,880
WONKA: Sign here.
SAM: Hold it.

673
00:48:39,960 --> 00:48:42,340
SAM: Let me through.
Violet, don't sign anything.

674
00:48:42,420 --> 00:48:45,550
- What's this all about?
- Standard formal contract.

675
00:48:45,630 --> 00:48:49,470
Don't talk to me about contracts,
I use them myself. They're for suckers.

676
00:48:49,560 --> 00:48:52,810
You wouldn't begrudge me
a little protection? A drop?

677
00:48:52,890 --> 00:48:54,600
I don't sign anything without my lawyer.

678
00:48:54,690 --> 00:48:56,480
MR. SALT: Veruca
don't sign anything, either.

679
00:48:56,560 --> 00:48:57,640
Then she don't go in.

680
00:48:57,730 --> 00:48:59,230
I'm sorry. Rules of the house.

681
00:48:59,310 --> 00:49:01,690
I want to go in. Don't you dare stop me.

682
00:49:01,780 --> 00:49:03,410
MR. SALT: I'm only trying to help you.

683
00:49:03,490 --> 00:49:07,620
Give me that pen.
You're always making things difficult.

684
00:49:08,910 --> 00:49:12,580
Nicely handled, Veruca.
She's a girl who knows where she's going.

685
00:49:13,370 --> 00:49:15,830
What's all that small print at the bottom?

686
00:49:15,910 --> 00:49:19,160
If you have any problems,
dial information. Thank you for calling.

687
00:49:22,250 --> 00:49:24,920
MRS. TEEVEE: I assume there's
an accident indemnity clause?

688
00:49:25,010 --> 00:49:26,180
Never between friends.

689
00:49:26,260 --> 00:49:30,220
Saw this in a movie once.
A guy signed his wife's insurance policy.

690
00:49:30,300 --> 00:49:32,180
- Then he bumped her off.
- Clever.

691
00:49:32,260 --> 00:49:35,890
- What about me, Grandpa?
- Sign away. We've got nothing to lose.

692
00:49:35,980 --> 00:49:37,610
VERUCA: Let's go in. Come on.

693
00:49:37,690 --> 00:49:40,990
Patience, little dear.
Everything has to be in order.

694
00:49:41,480 --> 00:49:43,480
Everyone signed? Yes?

695
00:49:43,650 --> 00:49:45,490
Good. On we go.

696
00:49:54,660 --> 00:49:56,250
WONKA: Ninety-nine...

697
00:49:56,460 --> 00:49:57,920
forty-four...

698
00:49:58,000 --> 00:49:59,920
100% pure.

699
00:50:01,500 --> 00:50:03,750
Just through the other door, please.

700
00:50:06,670 --> 00:50:10,260
MR. SALT: There's some mistake here.
MIKE: There is no other door.

701
00:50:10,510 --> 00:50:14,350
- There's no way out.
- I know there's a door here someplace.

702
00:50:14,850 --> 00:50:18,310
SAM: I don't like this, Wonka.
MR. SALT: Is this a trick or something?

703
00:50:18,440 --> 00:50:22,110
Mr. Wonka, help!
I'm getting squashed. Save me.

704
00:50:22,190 --> 00:50:24,530
Is it my soul that calls upon my name?

705
00:50:24,610 --> 00:50:26,450
Let me out or I'll scream!

706
00:50:26,530 --> 00:50:29,030
- Somebody's touching me.
- Now look here, Wonka!

707
00:50:29,110 --> 00:50:33,700
Excuse me, questions will come at the end of the session.
We must press on. Come along.

708
00:50:35,120 --> 00:50:37,080
WONKA: Here we are.
SAM: Ugh, don't be a damn fool. Wonka!

709
00:50:37,160 --> 00:50:38,830
That's the way we came in.

710
00:50:38,910 --> 00:50:41,040
It is? Are you sure?

711
00:50:41,210 --> 00:50:43,300
We've just come through there.

712
00:50:43,670 --> 00:50:45,260
How do you like that?

713
00:50:46,130 --> 00:50:48,420
WONKA: There we are.
MR. SALT: What is this, Wonka?

714
00:50:48,510 --> 00:50:51,350
- Some kind of funhouse?
- Why? Having fun?

715
00:50:51,430 --> 00:50:55,520
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm had enough!
I'm not going in there.
MR. BEAUREGARDE: Come on, Violet, we're getting out of here.

716
00:50:55,600 --> 00:50:59,100
You can't get out backwards.
You got to go forwards to go back.

717
00:50:59,180 --> 00:51:00,680
Better press on.

718
00:51:01,690 --> 00:51:04,280
MIKE: The room is getting smaller.
MRS. TEEVEE: No, it's not.

719
00:51:04,360 --> 00:51:07,030
MRS. TEEVEE: He's getting bigger.
MR. SALT: He's at it again.

720
00:51:07,110 --> 00:51:09,950
VIOLET: Where's the chocolate?
SAM: I doubt if there is any.

721
00:51:10,030 --> 00:51:12,410
I doubt if any of us
will get out of here alive.

722
00:51:12,490 --> 00:51:14,990
Oh, you should never never doubt
what nobody is sure about.

723
00:51:15,080 --> 00:51:17,370
You're not squeezing me
through that tiny door.

724
00:51:17,450 --> 00:51:20,200
You're off your nut.
No one can get through there.

725
00:51:20,290 --> 00:51:24,630
My dear friends, you are now
about to enter the nerve center...

726
00:51:24,960 --> 00:51:27,210
to the entire Wonka factory.

727
00:51:28,130 --> 00:51:31,930
Inside this room,
all of my dreams become realities.

728
00:51:32,130 --> 00:51:34,630
And some of my realities become dreams.

729
00:51:34,720 --> 00:51:38,470
And almost everything you'll see
is eatable, edible.

730
00:51:38,560 --> 00:51:40,770
I mean, you can eat almost everything.

731
00:51:40,850 --> 00:51:42,640
Let me in, I'm starving.

732
00:51:42,730 --> 00:51:45,860
Don't get overexcited.
Don't lose your head, Augustus.

733
00:51:45,940 --> 00:51:47,570
We wouldn't want anyone to lose that.

734
00:51:49,820 --> 00:51:50,950
Yet.

735
00:51:51,150 --> 00:51:52,940
WONKA: Now, the combination.

736
00:51:53,160 --> 00:51:55,160
This is a musical lock.

737
00:51:55,240 --> 00:51:57,490
[Lively piano music playing]

738
00:51:57,660 --> 00:51:58,870
Rachmaninoff.

739
00:51:59,080 --> 00:52:01,040
WONKA: Ladies and gentlemen...

740
00:52:01,500 --> 00:52:03,170
boys and girls...

741
00:52:04,170 --> 00:52:05,920
the Chocolate Room.

742
00:52:06,630 --> 00:52:10,090
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

743
00:52:43,910 --> 00:52:46,500
WONKA: Hold your breath. Make a wish.

744
00:52:46,750 --> 00:52:48,290
Count to three.

745
00:52:48,960 --> 00:52:52,460
[Singing] Come with me and you'll be

746
00:52:52,670 --> 00:52:56,340
In a world of pure imagination

747
00:52:56,890 --> 00:52:58,350
Take a look

748
00:52:58,550 --> 00:53:03,640
And you'll see into your imagination

749
00:53:09,980 --> 00:53:13,820
We'll begin with a spin

750
00:53:14,190 --> 00:53:17,900
Traveling in the world of my creation

751
00:53:18,240 --> 00:53:21,950
What we'll see will defy

752
00:53:23,160 --> 00:53:25,410
Explanation

753
00:53:27,960 --> 00:53:29,550
[Slow instrumental music]

754
00:53:35,800 --> 00:53:39,260
[Singing] If you want to view paradise

755
00:53:39,640 --> 00:53:42,850
Simply look around and view it

756
00:53:44,020 --> 00:53:47,110
Anything you want to, do it

757
00:53:48,100 --> 00:53:50,480
Want to change the world

758
00:53:51,730 --> 00:53:54,110
There's nothing to it

759
00:54:02,580 --> 00:54:05,250
SAM: Hurry up, Violet.
CHARLIE: This way, Grandpa.

760
00:54:12,130 --> 00:54:14,970
[Singing] There is no life i know

761
00:54:15,050 --> 00:54:18,220
To compare with pure imagination

762
00:54:18,300 --> 00:54:21,180
Living there you'll be free

763
00:54:21,600 --> 00:54:25,480
If you truly wish to be

764
00:55:06,640 --> 00:55:10,230
If you want to view paradise

765
00:55:10,690 --> 00:55:13,360
Simply look around and view it

766
00:55:14,310 --> 00:55:17,810
Anything you want to, do it

767
00:55:18,360 --> 00:55:20,950
Want to change the world

768
00:55:21,110 --> 00:55:25,410
There's nothing to it

769
00:55:37,130 --> 00:55:41,050
There is no life I know

770
00:55:42,550 --> 00:55:47,350
To compare with pure imagination

771
00:55:48,810 --> 00:55:53,940
Living there you'll be free

772
00:55:55,400 --> 00:55:58,990
If you truly

773
00:56:00,820 --> 00:56:04,740
Wish to be

774
00:56:16,080 --> 00:56:18,500
MRS. GLOOP: What a disgusting,
dirty river.

775
00:56:18,590 --> 00:56:19,930
It's industrial waste.

776
00:56:20,010 --> 00:56:22,430
You've ruined your watershed, Wonka.
It's polluted.

777
00:56:22,510 --> 00:56:25,100
- It's chocolate.
- That's chocolate?

778
00:56:25,180 --> 00:56:26,520
That's chocolate.

779
00:56:26,600 --> 00:56:28,190
A chocolate river.

780
00:56:28,260 --> 00:56:30,600
GRANDPA: That's the most fantastic thing
I've ever seen.

781
00:56:30,680 --> 00:56:32,520
WONKA: 10,000 gallons an hour.

782
00:56:32,770 --> 00:56:36,520
And look at my waterfall.
That's the most important thing.

783
00:56:37,610 --> 00:56:41,410
It's mixing my chocolate.
It's actually churning my chocolate.

784
00:56:41,610 --> 00:56:45,360
No other factory in the world
mixes its chocolate by waterfall.

785
00:56:45,780 --> 00:56:49,030
But it's the only way
if you want it just right.

786
00:56:49,200 --> 00:56:53,040
Grandpa, look over there across the river.
They're little men.

787
00:56:53,120 --> 00:56:55,040
GRANDPA: Jumping crocodiles, Charlie!

788
00:56:55,170 --> 00:56:57,380
Now we know who makes the chocolates.

789
00:56:57,460 --> 00:57:00,630
MR. SALT: I never saw anybody
with an orange face before.

790
00:57:01,260 --> 00:57:04,930
MR. SALT: Aren't they funny-looking?
MRS. TEEVEE: What are they doing there?

791
00:57:05,010 --> 00:57:06,970
WONKA: Creaming and sugaring time.

792
00:57:07,050 --> 00:57:10,300
VIOLET: They can't be real people.
WONKA: Of course they are.

793
00:57:10,390 --> 00:57:11,640
MR. SALT: Stuff and nonsense!

794
00:57:11,720 --> 00:57:13,430
No, Oompa Loompas.

795
00:57:14,770 --> 00:57:17,900
- From Loompaland.
- Loompaland? There's no such place.

796
00:57:17,980 --> 00:57:20,940
- Excuse me, dear lady-
- I am a geography teacher.

797
00:57:21,020 --> 00:57:24,400
Then you know all about it
and what a terrible country it is.

798
00:57:24,490 --> 00:57:27,620
Nothing but desolate wastes
and fierce beasts.

799
00:57:27,820 --> 00:57:30,990
The poor little Oompa Loompas
were so small and helpless...

800
00:57:31,080 --> 00:57:33,000
they would get gobbled up right and left.

801
00:57:33,080 --> 00:57:37,380
A Wangdoodle would eat 10 of them
for breakfast and think nothing of it.

802
00:57:37,500 --> 00:57:38,840
And so I said:

803
00:57:39,170 --> 00:57:41,960
" Come and live with me
in peace and safety...

804
00:57:42,170 --> 00:57:45,170
" away from all the Wangdoodles
and Hornswogglers...

805
00:57:45,260 --> 00:57:48,760
" and Snozzwangers
and rotten Vermicious Knids. "

806
00:57:48,840 --> 00:57:52,890
Snozzwangers? Vermicious Knids?
What kind of rubbish is that?

807
00:57:52,970 --> 00:57:55,850
I'm sorry, but all questions
must be submitted in writing.

808
00:57:55,930 --> 00:57:58,020
So, in the greatest of secrecy...

809
00:57:58,100 --> 00:58:01,770
I transported the entire population
of Oompa Loompas to my factory.

810
00:58:01,860 --> 00:58:05,860
I want an Oompa Loompa.
I want you to get me one right away.

811
00:58:05,940 --> 00:58:08,820
All right, I'll get you one
before the day's out.

812
00:58:08,950 --> 00:58:11,290
I want an Oompa Loompa now.

813
00:58:11,370 --> 00:58:12,870
VIOLET: Can it, you nit!

814
00:58:13,410 --> 00:58:15,160
AUGUSTUS: This stuff is terrific.

815
00:58:15,660 --> 00:58:18,870
CHARLIE: Look at Augustus.
GRANDPA: Don't worry, he can't drink it all.

816
00:58:19,290 --> 00:58:22,880
Augustus, sweetheart,
save some room for later.

817
00:58:23,130 --> 00:58:27,130
Please don't do that. My chocolate
must never be touched by human hands.

818
00:58:27,670 --> 00:58:30,720
Don't do that!
You're contaminating my entire river.

819
00:58:30,800 --> 00:58:32,970
Please, I beg you! Augustus!

820
00:58:33,060 --> 00:58:34,310
[Exclaiming]

821
00:58:34,390 --> 00:58:36,310
WONKA: My chocolate!
AUGUSTUS: Help!

822
00:58:37,980 --> 00:58:40,360
My chocolate. My beautiful chocolate.

823
00:58:41,610 --> 00:58:44,150
MRS. GLOOP: Don't just stand there.
Do something!

824
00:58:44,230 --> 00:58:46,480
Help. Police. Murder.

825
00:58:46,570 --> 00:58:49,820
GRANDPA: Quick, Charlie. Here.
CHARLIE: Quick, Augustus, grab this.

826
00:58:50,280 --> 00:58:51,740
MRS. TEEVEE: What's happening to him?

827
00:58:51,820 --> 00:58:54,160
MR. SALT: He's drowning.
MRS. GLOOP: Dive in. Save him!

828
00:58:54,240 --> 00:58:55,320
It's too late.

829
00:58:55,410 --> 00:58:58,040
- Too late?
- He's had it now. The suction's got him.

830
00:58:58,120 --> 00:58:59,910
MRS. GLOOP: Augustus, come back!

831
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:02,500
- Where is he?
- Watch the pipe.

832
00:59:03,000 --> 00:59:05,210
VERUCA: How long is he going to
stay down, Daddy?

833
00:59:05,300 --> 00:59:08,260
MRS. GLOOP: He can't swim.
WONKA: There's no better time to learn.

834
00:59:08,340 --> 00:59:11,340
MIKE: His coat's going up the pipe.
SAM: Call a plumber.

835
00:59:11,430 --> 00:59:13,520
MR. SALT: He's stuck in the pipe, isn't he?

836
00:59:13,600 --> 00:59:16,020
- It's his stomach that's done that.
- Help!

837
00:59:16,100 --> 00:59:19,020
VIOLET: He's blocking the chocolate.
GRANDPA: What happens now?

838
00:59:19,100 --> 00:59:23,350
The pressure will get him out.
It's building up behind the blockage.

839
00:59:23,980 --> 00:59:26,690
MR. SALT: How long is it going to take
to push through?

840
00:59:26,780 --> 00:59:30,240
The suspense is terrible. I hope it'll last.

841
00:59:30,320 --> 00:59:31,610
MR. SALT: Go on, boy.

842
00:59:33,370 --> 00:59:36,370
CHARLIE: He'll never get out.
GRANDPA: Yes, he will. Watch.

843
00:59:36,450 --> 00:59:39,830
Remember you once asked me
how a bullet comes out of a gun?

844
00:59:40,750 --> 00:59:43,300
He'll be made into marshmallows
in five seconds.

845
00:59:43,380 --> 00:59:46,720
- Impossible. That's absurd, unthinkable.
- Why?

846
00:59:47,050 --> 00:59:49,550
Because that pipe goes
to the Fudge Room.

847
00:59:49,630 --> 00:59:51,300
MRS. GLOOP: You terrible man.

848
00:59:53,720 --> 00:59:56,470
Take Mrs. Gloop to the Fudge Room,
but look sharp...

849
00:59:56,560 --> 00:59:58,770
or her little boy will get poured into the boiler.

850
00:59:58,850 --> 01:00:00,600
You boiled him up, I know it.

851
01:00:00,680 --> 01:00:04,600
Nil desperandum, dear lady. Across
the desert lies the Promised Land.

852
01:00:04,730 --> 01:00:06,230
WONKA: Goodbye, Mrs. Gloop.

853
01:00:06,320 --> 01:00:10,490
WONKA: Adieu. Auf Wiedersehen.
Gesundheit. Farewell.

854
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:14,660
[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

855
01:00:14,740 --> 01:00:17,990
I've got a perfect puzzle for you

856
01:00:18,080 --> 01:00:21,250
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Dee

857
01:00:21,330 --> 01:00:24,460
If you are wise you'll listen to me

858
01:00:24,670 --> 01:00:27,670
What do you get
when you guzzle down sweets?

859
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:31,000
Eating as much as an elephant eats?

860
01:00:31,260 --> 01:00:34,260
What are you at getting terribly fat?

861
01:00:34,430 --> 01:00:38,390
What do you think will come of that?

862
01:00:39,350 --> 01:00:41,140
I don't like the look of it

863
01:00:41,230 --> 01:00:44,020
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da

864
01:00:44,270 --> 01:00:47,360
If you're not greedy, you will go far

865
01:00:47,610 --> 01:00:50,530
You will live in happiness, too

866
01:00:50,940 --> 01:00:54,740
Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

867
01:01:04,460 --> 01:01:07,630
What kind of place
are you running here, anyhow, Wonka?

868
01:01:07,790 --> 01:01:09,210
[Speaking in French]

869
01:01:11,380 --> 01:01:12,800
What's he talking about?

870
01:01:21,970 --> 01:01:24,760
[Soft instrumental music]

871
01:01:31,820 --> 01:01:33,240
Wow, what a boat!

872
01:01:33,320 --> 01:01:35,200
Looks good enough to eat.

873
01:01:35,280 --> 01:01:38,070
That's quite a nice little canoe
you've got there, Wonka.

874
01:01:38,160 --> 01:01:42,210
" All I ask is a tall ship
and a star to sail her by"

875
01:01:42,410 --> 01:01:45,830
WONKA: All aboard, everybody.
MR. SALT: Ladies first. That means Veruca.

876
01:01:45,910 --> 01:01:48,830
If she's a lady, I'm a Vermicious Knid.

877
01:01:48,920 --> 01:01:52,760
- You sure this thing will float, eh. Wonka ?
- With your buoyancy, sir. Rest assured.

878
01:01:55,090 --> 01:01:58,930
Nothing to worry about, my dear lady.
I take good care of my guests.

879
01:01:59,010 --> 01:02:02,930
- Yeah, you took real good care of that August kid
..over there, yeah sure.
- Everybody aboard.

880
01:02:04,350 --> 01:02:06,230
You're going to love this.

881
01:02:06,440 --> 01:02:07,770
Just love it.

882
01:02:08,400 --> 01:02:10,030
[Bell ringing]

883
01:02:13,730 --> 01:02:15,900
[Lilting instrumental music]

884
01:02:38,300 --> 01:02:42,260
Daddy, I want a boat like this.
A beautiful paddle boat, that's what i want.

885
01:02:42,350 --> 01:02:45,020
What she wants
is a good kick in the pants.

886
01:02:45,100 --> 01:02:46,690
I'm going to be seasick.

887
01:02:46,770 --> 01:02:48,480
WONKA: Here, try one of this.
MRS. TEEVEE: What are they?

888
01:02:48,560 --> 01:02:51,270
WONKA: Rainbow drops.
Suck 'em, you can spit in seven colors.

889
01:02:51,360 --> 01:02:52,860
Spitting's a dirty habit.

890
01:02:52,940 --> 01:02:54,530
I know a worse one.

891
01:02:54,980 --> 01:02:57,650
[Lilting instrumental music continues]

892
01:03:06,410 --> 01:03:09,250
- What business are you in, Salt?
- Nuts.

893
01:03:19,930 --> 01:03:21,350
[Tense instrumental music]

894
01:03:21,430 --> 01:03:24,770
MR. SALT: Where are we going?
SAM: I don't know. But, i don't like the looks of that tunnel up there.

895
01:03:24,850 --> 01:03:26,100
-Hey Wonka, i want off.

896
01:03:26,180 --> 01:03:31,100
" Around the world and home again
That's the sailor's way! "

897
01:03:32,020 --> 01:03:34,360
VERUCA: I don't like this ride, Daddy.

898
01:03:34,440 --> 01:03:35,730
[All exclaiming]

899
01:03:35,820 --> 01:03:37,160
WONKA: Faster!

900
01:03:38,360 --> 01:03:41,910
MRS. TEEVEE: We're going too fast.
VIOLET: We're going to sink, I know it.

901
01:03:42,030 --> 01:03:44,280
VERUCA: Why doesn't he stop the boat?
WONKA: Faster!

902
01:03:44,370 --> 01:03:46,830
MR. SALT: Close your eyes
and hang on tight.

903
01:03:48,000 --> 01:03:50,750
[Tense instrumental music intensifies]

904
01:03:51,210 --> 01:03:53,920
- What is this, a freak-out?
- This isn't funny, Wonka!

905
01:03:54,040 --> 01:03:56,290
You can't possibly see where you're going.

906
01:03:56,380 --> 01:03:58,300
You're right. I can't.

907
01:03:58,550 --> 01:04:01,220
MIKE: Boy, what a great series
this would make.

908
01:04:02,380 --> 01:04:06,550
- This is kind of strange.
- Yes, strange, Charlie. But it's fun.

909
01:04:06,970 --> 01:04:08,600
CHARLIE: This is terrific.

910
01:04:08,890 --> 01:04:11,060
MR. SALT: I'd like to get off the boat,
Wonka!

911
01:04:12,600 --> 01:04:15,730
- I think I'm gonna be sick.
- This has gone too far.

912
01:04:15,810 --> 01:04:18,480
Tell that little guy to turn us around!

913
01:04:19,150 --> 01:04:20,940
Now I am going to be sick.

914
01:04:25,990 --> 01:04:27,830
[Screaming]

915
01:04:28,490 --> 01:04:32,080
[Singing] There's no earthly way
of knowing

916
01:04:32,160 --> 01:04:33,240
He's singing.

917
01:04:33,330 --> 01:04:36,330
[Singing] Which direction we are going

918
01:04:36,920 --> 01:04:40,760
There's no knowing where we're rowing

919
01:04:41,340 --> 01:04:44,340
Or which way the river's flowing

920
01:04:44,840 --> 01:04:49,390
Is it raining? Is it snowing?

921
01:04:49,680 --> 01:04:53,680
Is a hurricane a-blowing?

922
01:04:55,440 --> 01:04:59,190
Not a speck of light is showing
So the danger must be growing

923
01:04:59,780 --> 01:05:02,280
Are the fires of hell a-glowing?

924
01:05:03,110 --> 01:05:05,740
Is the Grizzly Reaper mowing?

925
01:05:05,950 --> 01:05:07,240
Yes!

926
01:05:07,450 --> 01:05:12,080
The danger must be growing
For the rowers keep on rowing

927
01:05:12,290 --> 01:05:15,290
And they're certainly not showing

928
01:05:15,580 --> 01:05:19,460
Any signs that they are slowing!

929
01:05:20,800 --> 01:05:22,930
[Screaming]

930
01:05:24,220 --> 01:05:26,390
Make him stop, Daddy!

931
01:05:26,550 --> 01:05:28,720
MR. SALT: It's gone far enough!
WONKA: Quite right.

932
01:05:28,800 --> 01:05:30,010
Stop the boat!

933
01:05:31,720 --> 01:05:33,390
WONKA: We're there.
MRS. TEEVEE: Where?

934
01:05:33,480 --> 01:05:34,650
Here.

935
01:05:34,730 --> 01:05:38,400
A small step for mankind,
but a giant step for us.

936
01:05:38,730 --> 01:05:42,280
- All ashore.
- Let me off this freight.

937
01:05:42,360 --> 01:05:45,740
MIKE: Why don't they show stuff like that
on TV?

938
01:05:45,820 --> 01:05:49,530
- What a nightmare.
- Daddy, I do not want a boat like this.

939
01:05:49,700 --> 01:05:50,660
CHARLIE: " Dairy Cream. "

940
01:05:54,750 --> 01:05:56,380
BOTH: " Hair cream"?

941
01:05:56,580 --> 01:05:59,500
[Wonka speaking in German]

942
01:05:59,790 --> 01:06:01,250
That's not French.

943
01:06:01,500 --> 01:06:03,880
[Continues speaking in German]

944
01:06:07,760 --> 01:06:09,720
I can't take much more of this.

945
01:06:11,760 --> 01:06:13,640
Der Inventing Room.

946
01:06:14,980 --> 01:06:17,520
Now remember, no messing about.

947
01:06:17,600 --> 01:06:20,440
No touching, no tasting, no telling.

948
01:06:20,520 --> 01:06:21,610
GRANDPA: No telling what?

949
01:06:21,690 --> 01:06:25,900
All of my most secret inventions
are cooking and simmering in here.

950
01:06:25,990 --> 01:06:29,910
Old Slugworth would give his false teeth
to get inside for five minutes.

951
01:06:29,990 --> 01:06:32,530
So don't touch a thing.

952
01:06:40,080 --> 01:06:41,960
[Electronic beeping]

953
01:06:58,390 --> 01:07:01,440
Inventing Room?
Looks more like a Turkish bath to me.

954
01:07:01,520 --> 01:07:04,520
Even if Slugworth did get in here,
he couldn't find anything.

955
01:07:04,610 --> 01:07:07,780
- You got a garbage strike going on?
- Who does your cleaning up?

956
01:07:07,860 --> 01:07:11,780
Shouldn't you wear rubber gloves?
You'll have the health inspectors after you.

957
01:07:11,870 --> 01:07:15,670
Invention, my dear friends,
is 93% perspiration...

958
01:07:15,790 --> 01:07:17,960
6% electricity...

959
01:07:18,040 --> 01:07:19,960
4% evaporation...

960
01:07:20,040 --> 01:07:22,460
and 2% butterscotch ripple.

961
01:07:22,540 --> 01:07:24,750
MRS. TEEVEE: That's 105%.

962
01:07:24,960 --> 01:07:26,210
MR. SALT: Any good?

963
01:07:27,590 --> 01:07:28,760
[Ln high-pitched voice] Yes.

964
01:07:29,130 --> 01:07:30,550
Excuse me.

965
01:07:32,970 --> 01:07:36,390
Time is a precious thing. Never waste it.

966
01:07:38,230 --> 01:07:41,980
- He's absolutely bonkers.
- That's not bad.

967
01:07:44,480 --> 01:07:47,940
WONKA: " In spring time
The only pretty ring time

968
01:07:48,030 --> 01:07:51,160
" Birds sing, hey ding a ding, ding

969
01:07:51,240 --> 01:07:54,790
" Sweet lovers love the spring"

970
01:07:54,870 --> 01:07:56,250
[Explosion]

971
01:07:58,200 --> 01:08:00,410
I told you not to, silly boy.

972
01:08:00,500 --> 01:08:01,920
Your teeth!

973
01:08:02,000 --> 01:08:03,540
Boy, that's great stuff.

974
01:08:03,960 --> 01:08:07,590
That's exploding candy for your enemies.
Great idea, isn't it?

975
01:08:07,670 --> 01:08:10,670
Not ready yet, though. Still too weak.
Needs more gelignite.

976
01:08:20,430 --> 01:08:21,810
What's that for?

977
01:08:21,940 --> 01:08:23,650
Gives it a little kick.

978
01:08:29,280 --> 01:08:31,410
Butterscotch? Buttergin?

979
01:08:31,530 --> 01:08:33,700
Got something going on inside, have you?

980
01:08:33,780 --> 01:08:36,700
WONKA: " Candy is dandy
but liquor is quicker. "

981
01:08:40,370 --> 01:08:42,870
MIKE: What's the matter?
Too hot, Mr. Wonka?

982
01:08:42,960 --> 01:08:45,130
Too cold. Far too cold.

983
01:08:45,210 --> 01:08:47,550
MR. SALT: That's gourmet cooking for you.

984
01:08:48,380 --> 01:08:50,420
No! Don't, please.

985
01:08:50,760 --> 01:08:53,260
Forgive me,
but no one must look under there.

986
01:08:53,340 --> 01:08:56,840
This is the most secret machine
in my entire factory.

987
01:08:57,050 --> 01:09:00,970
WONKA: This will sizzle old Slugworth.
CHARLIE: What does it do?

988
01:09:02,140 --> 01:09:04,310
Would you like to see?

989
01:09:06,690 --> 01:09:08,570
[Buzzing]

990
01:09:27,580 --> 01:09:29,080
CHARLIE: But what does it do?

991
01:09:29,170 --> 01:09:31,920
Can't you see?
It makes Everlasting Gobstoppers.

992
01:09:32,010 --> 01:09:34,100
VIOLET: Did you say
Everlasting Gobstoppers?

993
01:09:34,170 --> 01:09:37,590
For children with very little pocket money.
You can suck them forever.

994
01:09:37,680 --> 01:09:39,390
I want an Everlasting Gobstopper.

995
01:09:39,470 --> 01:09:40,850
- Me, too.
- And me.

996
01:09:40,930 --> 01:09:43,430
Fantastic invention.
Revolutionize the industry.

997
01:09:43,520 --> 01:09:47,270
You can suck them and suck them
and they'll never get any smaller. Never.

998
01:09:47,350 --> 01:09:48,600
At least, I don't think so.

999
01:09:48,690 --> 01:09:50,860
WONKA: Few more tests.
MIKE: How do you make them?

1000
01:09:50,940 --> 01:09:54,610
I'm a trifle deaf in this ear.
Speak a little louder next time.

1001
01:09:54,700 --> 01:09:56,370
WONKA: Who wants
an Everlasting Gobstopper?

1002
01:09:56,450 --> 01:09:58,120
CHILDREN: I do.

1003
01:09:59,120 --> 01:10:03,500
I can only give them to you
if you swear to keep them for yourselves...

1004
01:10:03,700 --> 01:10:08,080
and never show them to another
living soul as long as you all shall live.

1005
01:10:08,290 --> 01:10:10,170
WONKA: Agreed?
CHILDREN: Agreed.

1006
01:10:10,380 --> 01:10:14,010
Good. One for you,
and one for you, and one for you.

1007
01:10:14,090 --> 01:10:15,590
What about Charlie?

1008
01:10:15,720 --> 01:10:17,140
WONKA: And one for Charlie.

1009
01:10:17,220 --> 01:10:19,720
She's got two. I want another one.

1010
01:10:19,800 --> 01:10:22,720
VIOLET: Stop squawking, you twit!
WONKA: Everybody has had one.

1011
01:10:22,810 --> 01:10:26,060
And one is enough for anybody.
Now come along.

1012
01:10:27,770 --> 01:10:29,400
WONKA: Over here, if you'll follow me...

1013
01:10:29,810 --> 01:10:31,980
I have something rather special
to show you.

1014
01:10:32,070 --> 01:10:33,780
MR. SALT: It's special, all right.

1015
01:10:34,070 --> 01:10:36,410
I only hope my Veruca doesn't want one.

1016
01:10:36,490 --> 01:10:38,990
MIKE: What a contraption.
WONKA: Isn't she scrumptious?

1017
01:10:39,070 --> 01:10:42,450
She's my revolutionary,
non-pollutionary mechanical wonder.

1018
01:10:42,870 --> 01:10:45,410
Button, button, who's got the button?

1019
01:10:45,830 --> 01:10:47,330
CHARLIE: It's over there.

1020
01:10:47,920 --> 01:10:49,090
WONKA: Here?
CHARLIE: Yeah.

1021
01:10:49,540 --> 01:10:51,790
[Whizzing and whirring]

1022
01:11:05,180 --> 01:11:07,430
WONKA: What you are witnessing,
dear friends...

1023
01:11:07,520 --> 01:11:10,310
is the most enormous miracle
of the Machine Age.

1024
01:11:10,400 --> 01:11:13,320
The creation of a confectionery giant.

1025
01:11:14,650 --> 01:11:17,240
[Whizzing and whirring continues]

1026
01:11:23,280 --> 01:11:25,450
WONKA: Finito.
VERUCA: That's all?

1027
01:11:25,540 --> 01:11:27,540
Don't you know what this is?

1028
01:11:27,620 --> 01:11:29,500
VIOLET: By gum, it's gum!
WONKA: Wrong.

1029
01:11:29,580 --> 01:11:33,420
It's the most amazing, fabulous,
sensational gum in the whole world.

1030
01:11:33,500 --> 01:11:34,790
What's so fab about it?

1031
01:11:34,880 --> 01:11:38,130
- This tiny gum is a three-course dinner.
- Bull.

1032
01:11:38,220 --> 01:11:41,390
WONKA: Roast beef, it's not quite right yet.
VIOLET: I don't care.

1033
01:11:41,470 --> 01:11:43,560
I wouldn't do that. I really wouldn't.

1034
01:11:43,640 --> 01:11:45,480
So long as it's gum, that's for me.

1035
01:11:45,560 --> 01:11:47,810
Violet, don't do anything stupid.

1036
01:11:47,890 --> 01:11:49,890
What's it taste like?

1037
01:11:49,980 --> 01:11:52,820
Madness! It's tomato soup.

1038
01:11:52,980 --> 01:11:56,070
It's hot and creamy.
I can feel it running down my throat.

1039
01:11:56,150 --> 01:11:57,610
Stop. Don't.

1040
01:11:57,690 --> 01:12:01,860
- Why doesn't she listen to Mr. Wonka?
- Because, Charlie, she's a nitwit.

1041
01:12:01,950 --> 01:12:03,700
This sure is great soup.

1042
01:12:03,820 --> 01:12:05,740
The second course is coming up.

1043
01:12:05,830 --> 01:12:08,170
Roast beef and a baked potato.

1044
01:12:08,700 --> 01:12:10,450
With sour cream?

1045
01:12:11,670 --> 01:12:14,840
SAM: What's for dessert, baby?
VIOLET: Dessert? Here it comes.

1046
01:12:14,920 --> 01:12:18,920
Blueberry pie and cream.
It's the most marvelous pie I've tasted.

1047
01:12:19,010 --> 01:12:21,300
SAM: Holy Toledo!
What's happening to your face?

1048
01:12:21,380 --> 01:12:23,010
Cool it, Dad. Let me finish.

1049
01:12:23,090 --> 01:12:24,840
But your face is turning blue.

1050
01:12:24,930 --> 01:12:28,310
SAM: You're turning violet, Violet!
VIOLET: What are you talking about?

1051
01:12:28,390 --> 01:12:30,480
I told you I hadn't got it right yet.

1052
01:12:30,560 --> 01:12:33,190
You can say that again.
Look what it's done to my kid.

1053
01:12:33,270 --> 01:12:37,020
It always goes wrong
when we come to the dessert. Always.

1054
01:12:37,150 --> 01:12:39,110
Violet, what are you doing now?

1055
01:12:39,190 --> 01:12:41,110
- You're blowing up!
- I feel funny!

1056
01:12:41,200 --> 01:12:43,450
GRANDPA: I'm not surprised.
VIOLET: What's happening?

1057
01:12:43,530 --> 01:12:45,530
SAM: You're blowing up!
WONKA: Like a blueberry.

1058
01:12:45,620 --> 01:12:47,960
SAM: Call a doctor!
MRS. TEEVEE: Stick her with a pin.

1059
01:12:48,040 --> 01:12:50,540
CHARLIE: She'll pop.
WONKA: It happens every time.

1060
01:12:50,620 --> 01:12:53,790
- They all become blueberries.
- You've really done it this time!

1061
01:12:53,870 --> 01:12:55,540
I'll break you for this.

1062
01:12:55,630 --> 01:12:58,840
- Well, I'll get it right in the end.
- Help!

1063
01:12:59,630 --> 01:13:02,800
SAM: Let the air out of her quick.
WONKA: There's no air.

1064
01:13:02,880 --> 01:13:04,630
- That's juice.
- Juice?

1065
01:13:04,720 --> 01:13:07,930
Would you roll the young lady
down to the juicing room at once?

1066
01:13:08,010 --> 01:13:10,050
SAM: What for?
WONKA: For squeezing.

1067
01:13:10,140 --> 01:13:12,730
She has to be squeezed immediately
before she explodes.

1068
01:13:12,810 --> 01:13:15,400
- Explodes?
- It's a fairly simple operation.

1069
01:13:15,480 --> 01:13:19,230
[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1070
01:13:19,320 --> 01:13:22,780
I've got another puzzle for you

1071
01:13:22,990 --> 01:13:26,160
Oompa Loompa Doompada Dee

1072
01:13:26,740 --> 01:13:30,200
If you are wise you'll listen to me

1073
01:13:30,450 --> 01:13:33,660
Gum chewing's fine
When it's once in a while

1074
01:13:33,750 --> 01:13:37,590
It stops you from smoking
And brightens your smile

1075
01:13:37,670 --> 01:13:40,970
But it's repulsive, revolting, and wrong

1076
01:13:41,090 --> 01:13:45,260
Chewing and chewing all day long

1077
01:13:46,760 --> 01:13:48,760
The way that a cow does

1078
01:13:50,350 --> 01:13:53,940
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da

1079
01:13:54,020 --> 01:13:57,230
Given good manners, you will go far

1080
01:13:57,690 --> 01:14:00,860
You will live in happiness, too

1081
01:14:01,190 --> 01:14:05,440
Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1082
01:14:09,700 --> 01:14:13,120
I'll get even with you for this,
if it's the last thing I ever do!

1083
01:14:13,200 --> 01:14:15,790
I got a blueberry for a daughter.

1084
01:14:16,750 --> 01:14:20,550
" Where is fancy bred,
In the heart or in the head?"

1085
01:14:22,000 --> 01:14:23,540
Shall we roll on?

1086
01:14:28,090 --> 01:14:29,430
WONKA: Thank you.

1087
01:14:29,970 --> 01:14:34,560
Well, well, two naughty,
nasty little children gone.

1088
01:14:34,980 --> 01:14:38,400
Three good, sweet little children left.

1089
01:14:40,480 --> 01:14:43,230
WONKA: Hurry, please. Long way to go yet.

1090
01:14:46,820 --> 01:14:49,030
Wait a minute. I must show you this.

1091
01:14:49,160 --> 01:14:51,910
Lickable wallpaper for nursery walls.

1092
01:14:51,990 --> 01:14:53,870
Lick an orange, it tastes like an orange.

1093
01:14:53,950 --> 01:14:57,790
Lick a pineapple, it tastes like a pineapple.
Go ahead, try it.

1094
01:14:58,210 --> 01:15:00,090
MRS. TEEVEE: I got a plum.

1095
01:15:00,170 --> 01:15:03,840
CHARLIE: Grandpa, this banana's fantastic.
It tastes so real.

1096
01:15:04,050 --> 01:15:07,260
Try some more.
The strawberries taste like strawberries.

1097
01:15:07,340 --> 01:15:09,840
The snozzberries taste like snozzberries.

1098
01:15:09,930 --> 01:15:12,810
Snozzberries?
Whoever heard of a snozzberry?

1099
01:15:14,140 --> 01:15:16,560
" We are the music makers.

1100
01:15:16,890 --> 01:15:19,850
" And we are the dreamers of dreams. "

1101
01:15:21,310 --> 01:15:22,810
Come along.

1102
01:15:25,820 --> 01:15:28,410
Something very unusual in here.

1103
01:15:28,900 --> 01:15:32,200
WONKA: Bubbles, bubbles everywhere,
but not a drop to drink.

1104
01:15:32,280 --> 01:15:35,080
WONKA: Yet.
CHARLIE: What's it making, Mr. Wonka?

1105
01:15:35,160 --> 01:15:37,580
WONKA: Fizzy-Lifting drinks.
They fill you with gas...

1106
01:15:37,660 --> 01:15:41,790
and the gas is so lifting, that it lifts you
right off the ground like a balloon.

1107
01:15:41,880 --> 01:15:44,220
VERUCA: Isn't it high? Gosh!

1108
01:15:44,380 --> 01:15:48,430
- But I dare not sell it yet. It's too powerful.
- Let us try some, please.

1109
01:15:48,510 --> 01:15:52,890
No, absolutely not. There'd be children
floating around all over the place.

1110
01:15:53,100 --> 01:15:55,350
Come along, don't hang about.

1111
01:15:55,680 --> 01:15:58,100
You'll be wild about this next room.

1112
01:15:59,180 --> 01:16:01,850
Let's take a drink. Nobody's watching.

1113
01:16:01,940 --> 01:16:03,690
A small one won't hurt us.

1114
01:16:09,700 --> 01:16:11,030
Not bad.

1115
01:16:12,700 --> 01:16:13,950
Well?

1116
01:16:14,200 --> 01:16:15,950
Nothing's happening.

1117
01:16:16,030 --> 01:16:18,740
GRANDPA: You're right, Charlie.
I can't understand why.

1118
01:16:18,830 --> 01:16:20,210
[Grandpa shrieks]

1119
01:16:21,710 --> 01:16:23,420
I feel terribly strange.

1120
01:16:23,540 --> 01:16:26,380
- What do we do now?
- I don't know, Charlie.

1121
01:16:26,800 --> 01:16:29,600
We're in big trouble.
Mr. Wonka isn't going to like this.

1122
01:16:29,670 --> 01:16:31,380
We can't stay up here all day.

1123
01:16:31,470 --> 01:16:33,510
- You're right but-
- I'll try and get down.

1124
01:16:33,590 --> 01:16:36,590
All right, Charlie,
but please be very careful.

1125
01:16:37,810 --> 01:16:41,560
It's fun, Grandpa. It works.
Come on in, the air's fine.

1126
01:16:41,640 --> 01:16:44,730
- I haven't been swimming in 20 years.
- Give me your hand.

1127
01:16:44,810 --> 01:16:46,730
I don't think I ought to.

1128
01:16:46,860 --> 01:16:49,150
[Light instrumental music]

1129
01:16:49,400 --> 01:16:51,320
This is great!

1130
01:16:51,740 --> 01:16:55,200
CHARLIE: Try this, Grandpa.
GRANDPA: All right, Charlie. Wait for me.

1131
01:16:55,660 --> 01:16:59,120
[Grandpa and Charlie squeal with delight]

1132
01:16:59,580 --> 01:17:02,460
GRANDPA: I'm a shooting star.
CHARLIE: I'm a rocket!

1133
01:17:03,920 --> 01:17:05,800
This is really great.

1134
01:17:05,920 --> 01:17:08,210
Look, I'm a bird!

1135
01:17:10,090 --> 01:17:12,260
GRANDPA: I feel light as a feather.

1136
01:17:13,090 --> 01:17:16,010
Look down. We're really high now.

1137
01:17:20,520 --> 01:17:22,190
Watch this, Grandpa.

1138
01:17:25,770 --> 01:17:27,270
Wonderful, Charlie.

1139
01:17:27,440 --> 01:17:29,780
- Try it, Grandpa.
- I don't know.

1140
01:17:29,860 --> 01:17:32,490
- Come on, Grandpa.
- All right.

1141
01:17:35,360 --> 01:17:37,320
You did it, Grandpa!

1142
01:17:37,780 --> 01:17:38,860
[Grandpa groans]

1143
01:17:38,950 --> 01:17:40,870
I think I hit an air pocket.

1144
01:17:40,950 --> 01:17:43,120
You can fly to the moon this way.

1145
01:17:43,210 --> 01:17:44,960
GRANDPA: Let's just fly south
for the winter.

1146
01:17:45,040 --> 01:17:46,330
CHARLIE: Why not?

1147
01:17:48,540 --> 01:17:51,290
- I'm a bird!
- I'm a plane!

1148
01:17:51,380 --> 01:17:52,630
I'm...

1149
01:17:52,710 --> 01:17:54,550
going too high!

1150
01:17:55,380 --> 01:17:57,630
Grandpa, I can't get down!

1151
01:17:57,760 --> 01:18:00,640
Help! Grandpa, the fan!

1152
01:18:00,720 --> 01:18:03,060
GRANDPA: Stay away from it!
It'll chop us to bits!

1153
01:18:03,140 --> 01:18:07,060
GRANDPA: We're in trouble! I can't stop!
CHARLIE: It's pulling me in!

1154
01:18:07,150 --> 01:18:09,030
GRANDPA: I can't stop!

1155
01:18:09,230 --> 01:18:12,150
CHARLIE: What do we do?
GRANDPA: Grab hold of something quick!

1156
01:18:12,230 --> 01:18:14,820
CHARLIE: There's nothing
to grab on to! Help!

1157
01:18:15,110 --> 01:18:18,570
CHARLIE: We're gonna get killed!
GRANDPA: Help!

1158
01:18:18,660 --> 01:18:21,500
Mr. Wonka, please! Turn off the fan!

1159
01:18:22,450 --> 01:18:23,490
[Burping]

1160
01:18:25,040 --> 01:18:27,920
I'm going down. Quick, Charlie, burp!

1161
01:18:28,000 --> 01:18:30,250
If you don't, it'll cut you to ribbons!

1162
01:18:30,340 --> 01:18:32,050
CHARLIE: I can't! Help!

1163
01:18:32,130 --> 01:18:33,920
You've got to burp. It's the only way.

1164
01:18:34,670 --> 01:18:36,800
Attaboy. Burp again.

1165
01:18:37,180 --> 01:18:39,060
Attaboy. Come on.

1166
01:18:40,100 --> 01:18:41,140
[Charlie burping]

1167
01:18:41,350 --> 01:18:43,520
That's wonderful, Charlie.

1168
01:18:50,020 --> 01:18:53,320
GRANDPA: Grab on to me.
We're going to be all right now.

1169
01:18:53,570 --> 01:18:55,450
[Both burping]

1170
01:18:57,450 --> 01:19:00,160
[Light instrumental music]

1171
01:19:01,240 --> 01:19:02,450
Good boy.

1172
01:19:03,040 --> 01:19:05,500
From now on,
we keep our feet on the ground.

1173
01:19:05,580 --> 01:19:07,710
Let's catch up to the others.

1174
01:19:10,380 --> 01:19:14,220
I know what you're thinking.
They can't be doing what they're doing.

1175
01:19:14,420 --> 01:19:16,420
But they are. They have to.

1176
01:19:16,510 --> 01:19:19,300
I haven't met the Oompa Loompa yet
who could do it.

1177
01:19:19,380 --> 01:19:21,720
WONKA: These are the geese
that lay the golden eggs.

1178
01:19:21,800 --> 01:19:23,930
WONKA: They're larger
than ordinary geese.

1179
01:19:24,010 --> 01:19:28,140
WONKA: In fact, they're quadruple-size
geese which produce octuple-size eggs.

1180
01:19:28,230 --> 01:19:30,230
WONKA: They're laying overtime for Easter.

1181
01:19:30,310 --> 01:19:32,060
But Easter's over.

1182
01:19:32,480 --> 01:19:36,400
[Whispering] They don't know that.
I'm trying to get ahead for next year.

1183
01:19:37,740 --> 01:19:40,330
MR. SALT: What happens
if they drop one of those eggs?

1184
01:19:40,410 --> 01:19:43,000
WONKA: An omelet fit for a king, sir.

1185
01:19:43,330 --> 01:19:45,040
VERUCA: Are they chocolate eggs?

1186
01:19:45,160 --> 01:19:47,960
Golden chocolate eggs.
That's a great delicacy.

1187
01:19:48,160 --> 01:19:49,750
But don't get too close.

1188
01:19:49,830 --> 01:19:52,580
The geese are temperamental.
So we have the Eggdicator.

1189
01:19:52,670 --> 01:19:54,760
MRS. TEEVEE: Egg-di-what?
WONKA: The Eggdicator.

1190
01:19:54,840 --> 01:19:57,380
It can tell the difference
between a good egg...

1191
01:19:57,460 --> 01:19:58,710
and a bad egg.

1192
01:19:58,800 --> 01:20:02,600
If it's a good egg, it's shined up
and shipped out all over the world.

1193
01:20:02,760 --> 01:20:04,680
But if it's a bad egg...

1194
01:20:05,600 --> 01:20:07,100
down the chute.

1195
01:20:08,770 --> 01:20:10,150
[Horn honking]

1196
01:20:11,350 --> 01:20:14,810
- It's an educated Eggdicator.
- It's a lot of nonsense.

1197
01:20:15,360 --> 01:20:18,450
[Singing] A little nonsense now and then

1198
01:20:18,530 --> 01:20:21,870
Is relished by the wisest men

1199
01:20:22,360 --> 01:20:24,280
VERUCA: Daddy, I want a golden goose.

1200
01:20:24,530 --> 01:20:27,490
CHARLIE: Here we go again.
MR. SALT: All right, sweetheart.

1201
01:20:27,700 --> 01:20:30,040
You'll get one as soon as we get home.

1202
01:20:30,120 --> 01:20:32,460
No, I want one of those.

1203
01:20:33,130 --> 01:20:35,130
How much do you want for the goose?

1204
01:20:35,210 --> 01:20:37,550
WONKA: They're not for sale.
MR. SALT: Name your price.

1205
01:20:37,630 --> 01:20:39,800
WONKA: She can't have one.
VERUCA: Who says so?

1206
01:20:39,880 --> 01:20:43,550
- The man with the funny hat.
- I want one. I want a golden goose.

1207
01:20:44,050 --> 01:20:46,510
Gooses. Geeses.

1208
01:20:46,720 --> 01:20:49,470
I want my geese
to lay golden eggs for Easter.

1209
01:20:49,560 --> 01:20:51,900
MR. SALT: It will, dear.
VERUCA: At least 100 a day.

1210
01:20:51,980 --> 01:20:54,980
MR. SALT: Anything you say.
VERUCA: And by the way...

1211
01:20:55,150 --> 01:20:58,490
VERUCA: I want a feast.
MR. SALT: You ate before you came here.

1212
01:20:58,570 --> 01:21:00,320
[Singing] I want a bean-feast

1213
01:21:00,400 --> 01:21:01,490
One of those.

1214
01:21:01,570 --> 01:21:04,570
[Singing] Cream buns and doughnuts
And fruitcake with no nuts

1215
01:21:04,660 --> 01:21:06,410
So good you could go nuts

1216
01:21:06,490 --> 01:21:10,990
- You'll have them when you get home.
- No, now. I want a ball.

1217
01:21:11,580 --> 01:21:13,670
[Singing] I want a party

1218
01:21:13,920 --> 01:21:16,210
Pink macaroons and a million balloons

1219
01:21:16,290 --> 01:21:18,500
And performing baboons and...

1220
01:21:18,590 --> 01:21:19,880
Give it to me

1221
01:21:20,300 --> 01:21:21,430
Now.

1222
01:21:21,510 --> 01:21:23,680
[Singing] I want the world

1223
01:21:23,840 --> 01:21:26,180
I want the whole world

1224
01:21:26,350 --> 01:21:30,690
I want to lock it all up in my pocket
It's my bar of chocolate

1225
01:21:30,850 --> 01:21:33,850
Give it to me now

1226
01:21:34,020 --> 01:21:35,860
I want today

1227
01:21:36,360 --> 01:21:38,400
I want tomorrow

1228
01:21:38,610 --> 01:21:41,240
I want to wear them
Like braids in my hair

1229
01:21:41,440 --> 01:21:43,530
And I don't want to share them!

1230
01:21:47,370 --> 01:21:51,040
I want a party with roomfuls of laughter

1231
01:21:51,200 --> 01:21:54,040
Ten thousand tons of ice cream

1232
01:21:54,210 --> 01:21:57,920
And if I don't get the things I am after

1233
01:21:58,790 --> 01:22:02,750
I'm going to scream

1234
01:22:12,640 --> 01:22:14,060
I want the works

1235
01:22:14,140 --> 01:22:15,640
I want the whole works

1236
01:22:15,730 --> 01:22:18,400
Presents and prizes
And sweets and surprises

1237
01:22:18,560 --> 01:22:21,350
Of all shapes and sizes
And now

1238
01:22:21,480 --> 01:22:25,480
Don't care how
I want it now

1239
01:22:30,200 --> 01:22:31,540
[Horn honking]

1240
01:22:38,080 --> 01:22:39,830
She was a bad egg.

1241
01:22:41,210 --> 01:22:42,670
Where's she gone?

1242
01:22:42,750 --> 01:22:45,920
Where all the other bad eggs go.
Down the garbage chute.

1243
01:22:46,180 --> 01:22:48,180
The garbage chute?

1244
01:22:48,760 --> 01:22:51,560
- Where does it lead to?
- To the furnace.

1245
01:22:52,260 --> 01:22:54,100
The furnace?

1246
01:22:54,770 --> 01:22:57,860
- She'll be sizzled like a sausage.
- Not necessarily.

1247
01:22:57,940 --> 01:23:00,400
She could be stuck inside the tube.

1248
01:23:00,650 --> 01:23:01,780
Hold on!

1249
01:23:01,860 --> 01:23:04,950
MR. SALT: Veruca, sweetheart.
Daddy's coming!

1250
01:23:08,030 --> 01:23:10,200
There's gonna be a lot of garbage today.

1251
01:23:10,280 --> 01:23:13,160
Mr. Salt finally got what he wanted.

1252
01:23:13,370 --> 01:23:15,960
- What's that?
- Veruca went first.

1253
01:23:16,120 --> 01:23:20,000
CHARLIE: Mr. Wonka, they won't really
be burned in the furnace, will they?

1254
01:23:20,880 --> 01:23:23,800
I think that furnace is lit
only every other day.

1255
01:23:23,960 --> 01:23:26,550
So they have a good sporting chance.

1256
01:23:27,300 --> 01:23:30,800
[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1257
01:23:30,970 --> 01:23:34,470
I've got another puzzle for you

1258
01:23:34,640 --> 01:23:37,890
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Dee

1259
01:23:38,230 --> 01:23:41,650
If you are wise you'll listen to me

1260
01:23:41,900 --> 01:23:45,360
Who do you blame when your kid is a brat

1261
01:23:45,440 --> 01:23:48,820
Pampered and spoiled like a Siamese cat?

1262
01:23:48,900 --> 01:23:52,240
Blaming the kids is a lie and a shame

1263
01:23:52,320 --> 01:23:56,200
You know exactly who's to blame

1264
01:23:57,160 --> 01:23:59,660
The mother and the father

1265
01:24:01,000 --> 01:24:04,210
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da

1266
01:24:04,500 --> 01:24:08,000
If you're not spoiled then you will go far

1267
01:24:08,170 --> 01:24:12,130
You will live in happiness, too

1268
01:24:13,430 --> 01:24:17,600
Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1269
01:24:18,810 --> 01:24:21,770
WONKA: I can't understand.
The children are disappearing like rabbits.

1270
01:24:21,850 --> 01:24:25,100
Well, we still have each other.
Shall we press on?

1271
01:24:25,190 --> 01:24:27,280
Can't we sit down for a minute?

1272
01:24:27,360 --> 01:24:31,530
- The pace is killing me.
- Transportation has been arranged.

1273
01:24:36,200 --> 01:24:38,290
WONKA: Behold the Wonkamobile.

1274
01:24:38,370 --> 01:24:40,620
" A thing of beauty is a joy forever. "

1275
01:24:40,710 --> 01:24:43,550
WONKA: Places, please.
The dance is about to begin.

1276
01:24:43,670 --> 01:24:46,300
WONKA: Better grab a seat,
they're going fast.

1277
01:24:46,550 --> 01:24:49,050
GRANDPA: What's that
they're filling it up with?

1278
01:24:49,210 --> 01:24:52,130
Ginger ale, ginger pop,
ginger beer, beer bubbles...

1279
01:24:52,220 --> 01:24:55,640
bubbleade, bubble cola, double cola,
double-bubble burp-a-cola...

1280
01:24:55,720 --> 01:24:58,720
and all that crazy carbonated stuff
that tickles your nose.

1281
01:24:58,890 --> 01:25:01,980
Few people realize
the tremendous power in those things.

1282
01:25:02,060 --> 01:25:03,940
I'm sorry I asked.

1283
01:25:04,020 --> 01:25:07,320
[Whispering] You think Slugworth would
pay extra to know about this?

1284
01:25:07,400 --> 01:25:10,360
Just keep your eyes open
and your mouth shut.

1285
01:25:11,860 --> 01:25:14,650
WONKA: Everybody set?
CHARLIE: Is this gonna go fast?

1286
01:25:14,740 --> 01:25:17,490
It should. It's got more gas in it
than a politician.

1287
01:25:17,580 --> 01:25:21,540
Hold on tight. I'm going to open her up
and see what she can do.

1288
01:25:29,760 --> 01:25:31,760
WONKA: " Swifter than eagles...

1289
01:25:32,050 --> 01:25:34,090
" stronger than lions! "

1290
01:25:34,470 --> 01:25:35,970
[Screaming]

1291
01:25:42,060 --> 01:25:43,770
MIKE: It's getting in my eyes!

1292
01:25:44,390 --> 01:25:46,270
MRS. TEEVEE: It's seeping in my shoes!

1293
01:25:46,440 --> 01:25:49,110
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm soaked!
It'll never come out!

1294
01:25:49,400 --> 01:25:51,110
MIKE: It's sticking to my gun.

1295
01:25:52,950 --> 01:25:54,540
[Wonka singing]

1296
01:25:59,450 --> 01:26:02,660
My dress! My hair! My face!

1297
01:26:03,960 --> 01:26:06,800
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm sending you
the cleaning bill, Mr. Wonka.

1298
01:26:09,920 --> 01:26:11,800
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm dry cleaned.

1299
01:26:17,090 --> 01:26:18,680
Grandpa, what was that
we just went through?

1300
01:26:19,560 --> 01:26:20,850
Hsaw Aknow.

1301
01:26:20,930 --> 01:26:22,010
MRS. TEEVEE: Is that Japanese?

1302
01:26:22,100 --> 01:26:24,230
No, " Wonka Wash" spelled backwards.

1303
01:26:24,310 --> 01:26:26,560
WONKA: That's it. The journey's over.

1304
01:26:26,650 --> 01:26:30,610
GRANDPA: Finest bath I've had in 20 years.
CHARLIE: Let's do it again, Mr. Wonka.

1305
01:26:30,730 --> 01:26:34,070
- That's as far as it goes?
- Couldn't we have walked?

1306
01:26:34,570 --> 01:26:38,910
If the good Lord had intended us to walk,
he wouldn't have invented roller skates.

1307
01:26:38,990 --> 01:26:43,240
Would you all please put these on?
We have to be very careful.

1308
01:26:43,750 --> 01:26:46,090
There's dangerous stuff inside.

1309
01:26:48,710 --> 01:26:52,340
WONKA: Wonkavision. My very latest
and greatest invention.

1310
01:26:53,260 --> 01:26:55,600
MIKE: It's television.
WONKA: It's Wonkavision.

1311
01:26:55,670 --> 01:26:58,840
I suppose you all know
how ordinary television works.

1312
01:26:58,930 --> 01:27:01,520
- You photograph-
- I do. You photograph something...

1313
01:27:01,600 --> 01:27:04,230
then it's split into millions of pieces...

1314
01:27:04,310 --> 01:27:08,860
that go whizzing through the air
and down to your TV set in the right order.

1315
01:27:09,190 --> 01:27:12,440
You should open your mouth a little wider
when you speak.

1316
01:27:12,520 --> 01:27:15,060
So I thought,
" If they can do it with a photograph...

1317
01:27:15,150 --> 01:27:17,990
" why can't I do it with a bar of chocolate?"

1318
01:27:20,370 --> 01:27:24,670
WONKA: I shall now send this chocolate
bar from one end of the room to the other.

1319
01:27:24,750 --> 01:27:28,380
It has to be big, because when
you transmit something by television...

1320
01:27:28,460 --> 01:27:30,960
it always ends up smaller
on the other end.

1321
01:27:31,040 --> 01:27:32,710
Goggles on, please.

1322
01:27:34,760 --> 01:27:38,010
WONKA: Lights, camera, action!

1323
01:27:38,630 --> 01:27:39,880
[Screaming]

1324
01:27:40,140 --> 01:27:42,060
You can remove your goggles.

1325
01:27:42,390 --> 01:27:44,060
Where's the chocolate?

1326
01:27:44,140 --> 01:27:46,890
It's flying over our heads
in a million pieces.

1327
01:27:47,020 --> 01:27:48,610
[Electronic beeping]

1328
01:27:49,100 --> 01:27:50,770
WONKA: Now watch the screen.

1329
01:27:53,730 --> 01:27:55,070
WONKA: Here it comes.

1330
01:27:56,320 --> 01:27:57,780
WONKA: There it is.

1331
01:27:57,950 --> 01:28:01,040
- Take it.
- How can you take it? It's just a picture.

1332
01:28:01,110 --> 01:28:02,820
All right, you take it.

1333
01:28:03,990 --> 01:28:07,700
- It's real.
- Taste it. It's delicious. It's just smaller.

1334
01:28:10,420 --> 01:28:12,670
- It's perfect.
- It's unbelievable.

1335
01:28:12,750 --> 01:28:14,540
- It's a miracle.
- It's a TV dinner.

1336
01:28:14,630 --> 01:28:17,340
- It's Wonkavision.
- It could change the world.

1337
01:28:17,420 --> 01:28:19,170
Can you send other things?

1338
01:28:19,260 --> 01:28:21,600
MIKE: Not just chocolate.
WONKA: Anything you like.

1339
01:28:21,680 --> 01:28:24,100
- What about people?
- People?

1340
01:28:26,100 --> 01:28:28,810
I don't really know. I suppose I could.

1341
01:28:29,020 --> 01:28:32,020
Yes, I'm sure I could.
I'm pretty sure I could.

1342
01:28:32,100 --> 01:28:34,020
But it might have some messy results.

1343
01:28:34,110 --> 01:28:37,450
MIKE: Look! I'm going to be
the first person to be sent by television.

1344
01:28:37,530 --> 01:28:40,370
MRS. TEEVEE: Get away from that thing!
WONKA: Come back.

1345
01:28:40,450 --> 01:28:42,540
MIKE: Lights, camera, action!

1346
01:28:44,320 --> 01:28:45,820
[Electronic beeping]

1347
01:28:46,200 --> 01:28:47,580
Where are you?

1348
01:28:47,790 --> 01:28:49,920
GRANDPA: He's up there,
in a million pieces.

1349
01:28:50,000 --> 01:28:51,920
MRS. TEEVEE: Mike, are you there?

1350
01:28:52,000 --> 01:28:53,920
WONKA: No good shouting.
Watch the screen.

1351
01:28:54,000 --> 01:28:55,920
[Electronic beeping continues]

1352
01:28:56,880 --> 01:28:59,220
Mike? Why is he taking so long?

1353
01:28:59,300 --> 01:29:01,640
A million pieces take a long time
to put together.

1354
01:29:01,720 --> 01:29:04,560
MRS. TEEVEE: Where are they?
WONKA: There's something coming.

1355
01:29:04,640 --> 01:29:07,390
MRS. TEEVEE: Is it Mike?
WONKA: It's hard to tell but-

1356
01:29:07,890 --> 01:29:10,480
The little groover's getting smaller
by the minute.

1357
01:29:10,560 --> 01:29:14,770
MIKE: Look at me. I'm the first person
in the world to be sent by television.

1358
01:29:15,520 --> 01:29:17,940
Wow, what a wild trip that was.

1359
01:29:18,150 --> 01:29:20,820
It's the greatest thing
that's ever happened to me.

1360
01:29:20,900 --> 01:29:24,570
Am I coming in clear?
Mom, I said, am I coming in clear?

1361
01:29:24,660 --> 01:29:26,750
Great. He's completely unharmed.

1362
01:29:26,830 --> 01:29:28,920
You call that unharmed?

1363
01:29:28,990 --> 01:29:32,240
Wow, that was something.
Can I do it again?

1364
01:29:32,330 --> 01:29:34,080
MRS. TEEVEE: No, there'll be nothing left.

1365
01:29:34,170 --> 01:29:36,420
Don't worry about a thing, Mom.
I feel fine.

1366
01:29:36,500 --> 01:29:38,420
MIKE: I'm famous. I'm a TV star.

1367
01:29:38,500 --> 01:29:40,840
MIKE: Wait till the kids back home
hear about this.

1368
01:29:40,920 --> 01:29:42,590
MRS. TEEVEE: Nobody will.

1369
01:29:42,670 --> 01:29:45,670
Where are you taking me?
I don't want to go in there.

1370
01:29:45,760 --> 01:29:48,100
Be quiet. Well?

1371
01:29:49,100 --> 01:29:52,810
Fortunately, small boys
are extremely springy and elastic.

1372
01:29:52,940 --> 01:29:55,900
So I think we'll put him
in my special taffy-pulling machine.

1373
01:29:55,980 --> 01:29:57,020
That should do the trick.

1374
01:29:57,150 --> 01:29:58,690
To the Taffy-Pulling Room.

1375
01:29:58,770 --> 01:30:02,520
The boy is in his mother's purse.
But be extremely careful.

1376
01:30:02,610 --> 01:30:04,030
[Mrs. Teevee stuttering]

1377
01:30:04,530 --> 01:30:06,450
What's he saying?

1378
01:30:06,700 --> 01:30:09,290
No, I won't hold you responsible.

1379
01:30:10,540 --> 01:30:14,250
And now, my dearest lady,
it's time to say goodbye.

1380
01:30:14,460 --> 01:30:16,250
No, don't speak.

1381
01:30:16,460 --> 01:30:20,710
For some moments in life,
there are no words. Run along now.

1382
01:30:20,880 --> 01:30:23,590
[Mrs. Teevee mumbles incoherently]

1383
01:30:27,220 --> 01:30:29,100
Adieu.

1384
01:30:29,720 --> 01:30:32,390
" Parting is such sweet sorrow. "

1385
01:30:38,270 --> 01:30:40,310
[Slow instrumental music]

1386
01:30:41,400 --> 01:30:44,400
[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1387
01:30:44,990 --> 01:30:47,830
I've got another puzzle for you

1388
01:30:48,530 --> 01:30:51,200
Oompa Loompa Doompada Dee

1389
01:30:51,990 --> 01:30:54,910
If you are wise you'll listen to me

1390
01:30:55,540 --> 01:30:58,540
What do you get from a glut of TV?

1391
01:30:58,750 --> 01:31:02,000
A pain in the neck and an IQ of three

1392
01:31:02,500 --> 01:31:05,920
Why don't you try simply reading a book?

1393
01:31:06,010 --> 01:31:08,550
Or could you just not bear to look?

1394
01:31:08,640 --> 01:31:10,890
You'll get no

1395
01:31:13,220 --> 01:31:16,140
You'll get no commercials

1396
01:31:16,230 --> 01:31:20,400
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da

1397
01:31:20,730 --> 01:31:24,280
If you like reading, you will go far

1398
01:31:24,610 --> 01:31:27,860
You will live in happiness, too

1399
01:31:28,360 --> 01:31:29,610
Like the

1400
01:31:30,240 --> 01:31:31,410
Oompa

1401
01:31:32,240 --> 01:31:35,290
Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do

1402
01:31:38,960 --> 01:31:42,880
So much to do: Invoices, bills, letters.

1403
01:31:43,880 --> 01:31:46,380
I must answer that note from the Queen.

1404
01:31:47,220 --> 01:31:50,890
What's gonna happen to the other kids?
Augustus and Veruca?

1405
01:31:50,970 --> 01:31:54,220
My dear boy, I promise you
they'll be quite all right.

1406
01:31:54,310 --> 01:31:59,230
When they leave here, they'll be restored
to their normal, terrible old selves.

1407
01:31:59,980 --> 01:32:02,730
But maybe they'll be
a little bit wiser for the wear.

1408
01:32:02,820 --> 01:32:04,400
Don't worry about it.

1409
01:32:04,480 --> 01:32:06,900
What do we do now, Mr. Wonka?

1410
01:32:07,320 --> 01:32:11,200
I hope you enjoyed yourselves.
Excuse me for not showing you out.

1411
01:32:11,280 --> 01:32:14,700
Straight up the stairs, you'll find it.
I'm busy. A whole day wasted.

1412
01:32:14,790 --> 01:32:16,630
Goodbye to you both.

1413
01:32:18,000 --> 01:32:19,420
[Door closing]

1414
01:32:20,330 --> 01:32:23,500
What happened?
Did we do something wrong?

1415
01:32:24,090 --> 01:32:26,010
I don't know, Charlie.

1416
01:32:26,590 --> 01:32:28,680
But I'm going to find out.

1417
01:32:34,100 --> 01:32:35,890
[Clock ticking]

1418
01:32:46,320 --> 01:32:49,450
GRANDPA: Mr. Wonka?
WONKA: I am extraordinarily busy, sir.

1419
01:32:49,530 --> 01:32:52,370
I just wanted to ask about the chocolate.

1420
01:32:52,450 --> 01:32:55,870
The lifetime supply of chocolate.
For Charlie.

1421
01:32:56,370 --> 01:32:57,910
When does he get it?

1422
01:32:58,040 --> 01:33:00,170
WONKA: He doesn't.
GRANDPA: Why not?

1423
01:33:00,370 --> 01:33:02,500
WONKA: Because he broke the rules.

1424
01:33:02,710 --> 01:33:05,420
What rules?
We didn't see any rules, did we?

1425
01:33:05,500 --> 01:33:07,540
Wrong, sir!

1426
01:33:07,960 --> 01:33:11,550
Under Section 37-B
of the contract signed by him...

1427
01:33:11,630 --> 01:33:15,630
it states quite clearly that
all offers shall become null and void if...

1428
01:33:15,720 --> 01:33:19,060
And you can read it for yourself
in this photostat copy:

1429
01:33:19,140 --> 01:33:20,730
" I, the undersigned...

1430
01:33:20,810 --> 01:33:23,810
" shall forfeit all rights,
privileges, and licenses...

1431
01:33:23,900 --> 01:33:26,110
" herein contained," etc.

1432
01:33:26,320 --> 01:33:30,030
'"Fax mentis incendium
gloriae culpum, '"etc.

1433
01:33:33,410 --> 01:33:36,710
It's all there, black and white,
clear as crystal!

1434
01:33:36,910 --> 01:33:39,120
You stole Fizzy-Lifting drinks!

1435
01:33:39,330 --> 01:33:43,080
You bumped into the ceiling which
now has to be washed and sterilized!

1436
01:33:43,170 --> 01:33:45,210
So you get nothing!

1437
01:33:45,420 --> 01:33:46,710
You lose!

1438
01:33:46,920 --> 01:33:48,710
Good day, sir!

1439
01:33:49,840 --> 01:33:51,430
You're a crook.

1440
01:33:51,510 --> 01:33:54,510
You're a cheat and a swindler,
that's what you are.

1441
01:33:54,590 --> 01:33:56,680
How can you do a thing like this?

1442
01:33:56,760 --> 01:34:00,510
Build up a little boy's hopes and
then smash all his dreams to pieces.

1443
01:34:00,600 --> 01:34:02,640
You're an inhuman monster!

1444
01:34:02,730 --> 01:34:04,400
I said, good day!

1445
01:34:08,610 --> 01:34:11,490
Come on, Charlie. Let's get out of here.

1446
01:34:11,570 --> 01:34:14,530
I'll get even with him
if it's the last thing I ever do.

1447
01:34:14,610 --> 01:34:17,530
If Slugworth wants a Gobstopper,
he'll get one.

1448
01:34:40,510 --> 01:34:41,930
Mr. Wonka.

1449
01:34:50,400 --> 01:34:54,860
WONKA: " So shines a good deed
in a weary world. "

1450
01:34:56,610 --> 01:34:57,650
Charlie.

1451
01:34:59,450 --> 01:35:01,080
My boy.

1452
01:35:01,740 --> 01:35:03,030
You've won!

1453
01:35:03,250 --> 01:35:05,250
You did it!

1454
01:35:05,330 --> 01:35:08,040
I just knew you would!

1455
01:35:09,630 --> 01:35:12,630
Forgive me for putting you through this.
Please forgive me.

1456
01:35:12,710 --> 01:35:15,840
Come in, Mr. Wilkinson.
Charlie, meet Mr. Wilkinson.

1457
01:35:15,920 --> 01:35:17,510
WILKINSON: Pleasure.
CHARLIE: Slugworth.

1458
01:35:17,590 --> 01:35:20,260
No, that's not Slugworth.
He works for me.

1459
01:35:20,350 --> 01:35:22,770
CHARLIE: For you?
WONKA: I had to test you, Charlie.

1460
01:35:22,850 --> 01:35:25,810
And you passed the test. You won!

1461
01:35:25,890 --> 01:35:28,390
GRANDPA: Won what?
WONKA: The jackpot, my dear sir.

1462
01:35:28,480 --> 01:35:31,190
WONKA: The grand and glorious jackpot.
CHARLIE: The chocolate?

1463
01:35:31,270 --> 01:35:34,650
The chocolate, yes.
But that's just the beginning.

1464
01:35:34,740 --> 01:35:38,450
We have to get on.
We have so much time and so little to do.

1465
01:35:38,530 --> 01:35:40,410
WONKA: Strike that. Reverse it.

1466
01:35:40,620 --> 01:35:42,250
This way, please.

1467
01:35:42,780 --> 01:35:44,870
We'll take the Wonkavator.

1468
01:35:45,410 --> 01:35:47,910
Step in, Charlie. Grandpa Joe, sir.

1469
01:35:55,960 --> 01:36:00,050
- This is the great glass Wonkavator.
- It's an elevator.

1470
01:36:00,140 --> 01:36:02,640
It's a Wonkavator.
An elevator only goes up and down.

1471
01:36:02,720 --> 01:36:06,270
But the Wonkavator goes sideways,
slantways, longways, back ways...

1472
01:36:06,350 --> 01:36:09,730
CHARLIE: And front ways?
WONKA:... and any other ways you think of.

1473
01:36:10,060 --> 01:36:14,360
It can take you to any room by pressing
one of these buttons. Any button.

1474
01:36:14,570 --> 01:36:17,240
Press a button and zing, you're off.

1475
01:36:17,990 --> 01:36:20,660
And up until now, I've pressed them all.

1476
01:36:20,910 --> 01:36:22,330
Except one.

1477
01:36:23,160 --> 01:36:24,540
This one.

1478
01:36:25,410 --> 01:36:27,580
WONKA: Go ahead, Charlie.
CHARLIE: Me?

1479
01:36:30,920 --> 01:36:32,170
There it goes.

1480
01:36:34,340 --> 01:36:35,680
Hold on tight.

1481
01:36:36,170 --> 01:36:39,010
I'm not exactly sure
what's going to happen.

1482
01:36:39,760 --> 01:36:43,180
Faster. If we don't pick up speed,
we won't get through.

1483
01:36:43,260 --> 01:36:44,970
CHARLIE: Get through what?

1484
01:36:45,470 --> 01:36:47,680
- You mean, we're going...
- Up and out.

1485
01:36:47,770 --> 01:36:50,900
But the roof is glass.
It'll shatter into a thousand pieces.

1486
01:36:50,980 --> 01:36:53,400
- We'll be cut to ribbons.
- Probably.

1487
01:36:57,110 --> 01:36:58,780
Hold on, everybody.

1488
01:36:59,950 --> 01:37:02,290
Here it comes.

1489
01:37:06,330 --> 01:37:08,540
[Light instrumental music]

1490
01:37:17,170 --> 01:37:20,970
GRANDPA: You did it. Congratulations.
WONKA: Get up, take a look.

1491
01:37:21,050 --> 01:37:23,720
Grandpa, our town looks so pretty
from up here.

1492
01:37:23,800 --> 01:37:26,970
GRANDPA: Look over here, Charlie.
I think I see our house.

1493
01:37:28,810 --> 01:37:30,810
GRANDPA: It really looks beautiful.

1494
01:37:30,890 --> 01:37:32,850
There's my school, Grandpa.

1495
01:37:33,230 --> 01:37:36,030
[Light instrumental music continues]

1496
01:37:38,780 --> 01:37:41,330
How did you like the chocolate factory?

1497
01:37:41,400 --> 01:37:44,320
I think it's the most wonderful place
in the whole world.

1498
01:37:44,410 --> 01:37:46,580
WONKA: I'm very pleased
to hear you say that...

1499
01:37:46,660 --> 01:37:48,950
because I'm giving it to you.

1500
01:37:50,080 --> 01:37:52,500
CHARLIE: What?
WONKA: That's all right, isn't it?

1501
01:37:52,580 --> 01:37:55,420
GRANDPA: You're giving Charlie-
WONKA: I can't go on forever.

1502
01:37:55,500 --> 01:37:57,750
WONKA: And I don't really want to try.

1503
01:37:57,840 --> 01:38:00,890
So who can I trust
to run the factory when I leave...

1504
01:38:00,960 --> 01:38:02,920
and take care
of the Oompa Loompas for me?

1505
01:38:03,010 --> 01:38:04,430
Not a grownup.

1506
01:38:04,510 --> 01:38:07,930
A grownup would want to do everything
his own way, not mine.

1507
01:38:08,010 --> 01:38:11,760
That's why I decided a long time ago
that I had to find a child.

1508
01:38:11,850 --> 01:38:15,270
A very honest, loving child...

1509
01:38:15,770 --> 01:38:19,270
to whom I can tell all my most precious
candy-making secrets.

1510
01:38:19,360 --> 01:38:22,030
CHARLIE: So, you sent the Golden Tickets?
WONKA: Right.

1511
01:38:22,110 --> 01:38:24,780
The factory's yours.
You can move in immediately.

1512
01:38:24,860 --> 01:38:26,860
GRANDPA: And me?
WONKA: Absolutely.

1513
01:38:27,030 --> 01:38:29,620
CHARLIE: What happens to the-
WONKA: The whole family.

1514
01:38:29,700 --> 01:38:31,660
I want you to bring them all.

1515
01:38:35,210 --> 01:38:36,960
But, Charlie...

1516
01:38:37,460 --> 01:38:41,420
don't forget what happened to the man
who suddenly got everything he wanted.

1517
01:38:41,510 --> 01:38:42,890
CHARLIE: What happened?

1518
01:38:42,970 --> 01:38:45,310
He lived happily ever after.

1519
01:38:47,220 --> 01:38:49,890
[Sweeping instrumental music]

1520
01:39:42,320 --> 01:39:43,360
English - SDH
